/* Black history roster: one lesson per weekday (WHO/WHAT/WHEN/WHY/HOW), 10 multiple-choice questions each, 4 options. First 12 are the basics. Review for accuracy before use. */
window.BLACK_HISTORY=[{"id":"douglass","name":"Frederick Douglass","years":"1818-1895","era":"past","tagline":"Escaped slavery and became a powerful voice for freedom","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Douglass: Words Were His Weapon","paras":["Frederick Douglass was born into slavery in Talbot County, Maryland, around 1818. His birth name was Frederick Bailey, and no one knows his exact birthday. As a boy in Baltimore, he began learning to read. When the lessons were stopped, he kept learning on his own.","Douglass escaped slavery on September 3, 1838, and settled in New Bedford, Massachusetts. He became a traveling speaker against slavery, and crowds were amazed by his clear, powerful words. In 1845, he published a book about his life. Later he started his own newspaper, the North Star.","Douglass escaped slavery in 1838 and published his book in 1845. He then spent about two years in Britain and returned in 1847 to start the North Star. The Civil War began in 1861, and he met President Abraham Lincoln at the White House in 1863. Douglass died in Washington, D.C., on February 20, 1895.","Douglass knew how cruel slavery was because he lived it. In many places in the South, teaching enslaved people to read was against the law. He shared his story because many people did not know what slavery was really like. After slavery ended in 1865, he kept fighting for the right to vote.","Douglass used words as his tools. He taught himself to read, studied a book of speeches called The Columbian Orator, and became one of the best speakers of his time. He also wrote books and ran a newspaper, so his message reached far beyond any one room, and his home, Cedar Hill, is now a national historic site. For an athlete, the lesson is to practice even when no one is helping you."],"remember":"Douglass taught himself to read and used words to fight for freedom.","quiz":[["Where was Douglass born?","Talbot County, Maryland","Rochester, New York","Richmond, Virginia","Boston, Massachusetts",0],["What was his name at birth?","Frederick Bailey","Frederick Auld","Frederick Johnson","Frederick Garrison",0],["What was the name of the newspaper Douglass started?","The North Star","The Liberator","The Rochester Times","The Freedom Herald",1],["Where did Douglass settle after he escaped?","New Bedford, Massachusetts","Boston, Massachusetts","Philadelphia, Pennsylvania","Albany, New York",1],["Which president did Douglass meet at the White House in 1863?","Abraham Lincoln","Andrew Johnson","Ulysses S. Grant","James Buchanan",2],["In what year did Douglass return from Britain and start the North Star?","1847","1838","1852","1861",2],["What was against the law in many places in the South?","Teaching enslaved people to read","Printing anti-slavery newspapers","Speaking at public meetings","Traveling north by train",3],["What did Douglass keep fighting for after slavery ended in 1865?","The right to vote","The right to a trial","The right to free land","The right to a pension",3],["What book of speeches did Douglass study?","The Columbian Orator","The Liberator Reader","Common Sense","Poor Richard's Almanack",4],["What is the name of his home that is now a national historic site?","Cedar Hill","Pine Ridge","Elm Grove","Oak Manor",4]]}]},{"id":"robinson","name":"Jackie Robinson","years":"1919-1972","era":"past","tagline":"Broke baseball's color line and met hate with courage","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Jackie Robinson Breaks the Color Line","paras":["Jackie Robinson was born on January 31, 1919, in Cairo, Georgia, the youngest of five children. In 1920, his mother, Mallie, moved the family to Pasadena, California. At UCLA, he became the first athlete in school history to earn varsity letters in four sports. Those sports were baseball, basketball, football, and track.","On April 15, 1947, Robinson played first base for the Brooklyn Dodgers. That day he broke baseball's color line, the unwritten rule that kept Black players out of the major leagues. He won the first Rookie of the Year award that season. In 1949, he won the Most Valuable Player award.","In 1945, Robinson played for the Kansas City Monarchs of the Negro Leagues. In 1946, he starred for the Montreal Royals, and in 1947 he joined the Dodgers. The Dodgers won the World Series in 1955, and Robinson retired in 1957. He died on October 24, 1972, at age 53.","Before 1947, Black players were kept out of the major leagues and played in the Negro Leagues instead. Dodgers general manager Branch Rickey wanted to change that. He chose Robinson because he needed one who could handle insults without fighting back. Robinson agreed, knowing other Black players were counting on him.","Robinson let his play do the talking. He was fast, a daring base runner who stole home 19 times in his career. He also had help, including his wife, Rachel, and Dodgers shortstop Pee Wee Reese. For an athlete, his lesson is that you cannot control every insult, but you can control how you react."],"remember":"Robinson stayed calm under pressure and let his play do the talking.","quiz":[["Where was Jackie Robinson born?","Cairo, Georgia","Pasadena, California","Atlanta, Georgia","Kansas City, Missouri",0],["In how many sports did he earn varsity letters at UCLA?","Four","Three","Five","Two",0],["Which team did Robinson play for when he broke the color line?","Brooklyn Dodgers","Boston Red Sox","New York Yankees","St. Louis Cardinals",1],["Which award did Robinson win in 1949?","Most Valuable Player","Rookie of the Year","Batting Triple Crown","Gold Glove Award",1],["Which Negro Leagues team did Robinson play for in 1945?","Kansas City Monarchs","Homestead Grays","Newark Eagles","Birmingham Black Barons",2],["In what year did the Dodgers win the World Series?","1955","1947","1949","1957",2],["Who was the Dodgers general manager who chose Robinson?","Branch Rickey","Pee Wee Reese","Walter O'Malley","Leo Durocher",3],["What kind of player did Rickey need?","One who could handle insults without fighting back","One who could hit more home runs than anyone","One who could throw the fastest pitches","One with years of major league experience",3],["How many times did Robinson steal home in his career?","19","9","29","42",4],["Which Dodgers shortstop helped support Robinson?","Pee Wee Reese","Duke Snider","Roy Campanella","Gil Hodges",4]]}]},{"id":"johnson","name":"Katherine Johnson","years":"1918-2020","era":"past","tagline":"NASA mathematician whose careful math helped send Americans into space","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Katherine Johnson: Every Number Counts","paras":["Katherine Johnson was born on August 26, 1918, in White Sulphur Springs, West Virginia. She loved numbers and was so good at school that she skipped several grades. At age 18, she enrolled at West Virginia State College and graduated in 1937 with highest honors. She earned a degree in mathematics and French.","Johnson was a NASA mathematician whose work guided spacecraft. In May 1961, she did the trajectory analysis for Alan Shepard, the first American in space. A trajectory is the path a spacecraft follows. In 1962, John Glenn asked her to check the computer's numbers by hand before he orbited Earth.","In the summer of 1953, Johnson began working at the Langley laboratory in Virginia. In 1958, NACA became NASA. Her math helped plan Apollo 11, the first Moon landing, in 1969, and in 1970 her backup charts helped guide Apollo 13 home. She retired in 1986 after 33 years at Langley.","Early spaceflights were dangerous because nobody had done them before. A small math mistake could send a capsule to the wrong place, so every number had to be exact. In 1962, computers were new and not everyone trusted them yet. Johnson also worked during segregation, when Black Americans and women were often denied chances.","Johnson's method was careful checking: she did not simply trust the machine, and she made sure the math was right before anyone's life depended on it. She began in West Area Computing, a group of Black mathematicians led by Dorothy Vaughan. In 2015, President Barack Obama gave her the Presidential Medal of Freedom. For an athlete, the lesson is to prepare so well that others can trust you when it counts."],"remember":"Katherine Johnson double-checked the math so astronauts could trust it.","quiz":[["Where was Katherine Johnson born?","White Sulphur Springs, West Virginia","Hampton, Virginia","Charleston, West Virginia","Richmond, Virginia",0],["What two subjects did she earn her degree in?","Mathematics and French","Physics and Spanish","Chemistry and English","History and Latin",0],["Whose 1961 flight did Johnson do trajectory analysis for?","Alan Shepard","John Glenn","Neil Armstrong","Gus Grissom",1],["What did John Glenn ask Johnson to do before his flight?","Check the computer's numbers by hand","Build the capsule's heat shield","Write the flight's radio script","Pick the rocket's launch time",1],["In what year did Johnson begin working at Langley?","1953","1943","1958","1962",2],["How many years did Johnson work at Langley?","33 years","23 years","43 years","13 years",2],["What could a small math mistake do?","Send a capsule to the wrong place","Make a capsule too heavy to launch","Cause a capsule radio to fail","Stop a capsule clock from working",3],["What was new in 1962 that not everyone trusted yet?","Computers","Rockets","Radar","Radios",3],["Who led West Area Computing?","Dorothy Vaughan","Mary Jackson","Margot Lee Shetterly","Sally Ride",4],["Which president gave her the Presidential Medal of Freedom?","Barack Obama","John F. Kennedy","Dwight Eisenhower","Ronald Reagan",4]]}]},{"id":"marshall","name":"Thurgood Marshall","years":"1908-1993","era":"past","tagline":"Lawyer who won Brown v. Board and became the first Black Supreme Court justice","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Thurgood Marshall: Law as a Tool","paras":["Thurgood Marshall was born on July 2, 1908, in Baltimore, Maryland. His father was a waiter, and his mother was a teacher. The University of Maryland law school rejected him because he was Black, so he went to Howard University School of Law. There, a teacher named Charles Hamilton Houston became his mentor.","Marshall became one of the greatest lawyers in American history. He argued 32 cases before the Supreme Court and won 29. His most famous case was Brown v. Board of Education, decided on May 17, 1954. The Court ruled that separate public schools for Black and white students were not equal.","In 1935, Marshall and Houston sued the University of Maryland for a rejected student named Donald Murray. In 1944, Marshall won Smith v. Allwright, a voting case. The Brown decision came in 1954. President Lyndon Johnson chose him for the Supreme Court in 1967, and he retired in 1991.","In Marshall's time, many places had laws that kept Black and white people apart. This was called segregation. Black children often went to separate schools that had fewer resources. Marshall argued that these rules broke the 14th Amendment, which says states must give every person equal protection under the law.","Marshall prepared hard, learning from Houston to study the facts and the law until he knew them well. He chose cases with a plan, starting with cases about graduate and law schools before the case about public schools. He led a team of lawyers at the NAACP Legal Defense Fund. For an athlete, the lesson is to study your game, trust your team, and keep working after a loss."],"remember":"Marshall prepared hard, picked his cases with a plan, and changed American law.","quiz":[["Where was Thurgood Marshall born?","Baltimore, Maryland","Washington, D.C.","Philadelphia, Pennsylvania","Richmond, Virginia",0],["Which law school did Marshall attend instead?","Howard University School of Law","Harvard Law School","Yale Law School","Columbia Law School",0],["How many Supreme Court cases did Marshall win?","29","32","19","35",1],["What did the Court rule in Brown v. Board of Education?","Separate public schools were not equal","States could set their own school rules","Schools must hire more Black teachers","Public colleges must be free for all",1],["In what year did Marshall win Smith v. Allwright?","1944","1935","1954","1967",2],["Which president chose Marshall for the Supreme Court?","Lyndon Johnson","Harry Truman","Dwight Eisenhower","Richard Nixon",2],["What was the system of laws that kept Black and white people apart called?","Segregation","Integration","Reconstruction","Emancipation",3],["Which amendment says states must give every person equal protection?","The 14th Amendment","The 1st Amendment","The 5th Amendment","The 19th Amendment",3],["Which group's lawyers did Marshall lead?","NAACP Legal Defense Fund","American Bar Association","Maryland State Bar","Howard Law Alumni Club",4],["What kind of cases did Marshall choose to start with?","Cases about graduate and law schools","Cases about housing and parks","Cases about trains and buses","Cases about prisons and jails",4]]}]},{"id":"morgan","name":"Garrett Morgan","years":"1877-1963","era":"past","tagline":"Inventor of a life-saving safety hood and an improved traffic signal","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Garrett Morgan: Inventor Who Acted","paras":["Garrett Morgan was born on March 4, 1877, in Kentucky. His parents had both been enslaved earlier in their lives, and he was one of eleven children. He left school after about the sixth grade but hired a tutor to keep learning. In 1895, he moved to Cleveland, Ohio, and got a job fixing sewing machines.","Morgan invented a safety hood and an improved traffic signal. The hood had a long tube that reached toward the floor, where the air is cleaner during a fire. His T-shaped traffic signal, patented in 1923, added a caution position, like today's yellow light.","Morgan received a patent for his safety hood in 1914. In 1916, an explosion trapped workers in a tunnel under Lake Erie, and Morgan and helpers wore hoods to rescue men. In 1923, he patented his traffic signal and later sold the rights to General Electric for $40,000. Morgan died in Cleveland on July 27, 1963, at age 86.","Fires and gas explosions could fill a room with smoke, and workers often had no good way to breathe. Morgan knew that smoke rises while cleaner air stays low, so he designed his hood to pull air from near the floor. Early traffic signals had only stop and go, which could cause crashes. Morgan wanted drivers to have a pause between stop and go.","Morgan learned by doing and began with real problems. He protected his ideas with patents. Today's yellow lights follow his caution idea, and his hood is seen as a step toward the modern gas mask. For an athlete, the lesson is to find a real problem, work on it, and be ready to act when your team needs you."],"remember":"Morgan solved real problems and acted when it counted.","quiz":[["How many children were in Morgan's family?","Eleven","Seven","Nine","Fifteen",0],["What job did Morgan get in Cleveland?","Fixing sewing machines","Building railroad cars","Driving a delivery wagon","Working in a steel mill",0],["Where did the safety hood's tube reach?","Toward the floor","Above the head","Into a water tank","Out a window",1],["What did Morgan's traffic signal add?","A caution position","A flashing red light","A walking signal","A turn arrow",1],["Where were workers trapped by the 1916 explosion?","In a tunnel under Lake Erie","In a coal mine in Kentucky","In a factory in Cleveland","In a steel mill in Ohio",2],["Which company bought the rights to his traffic signal?","General Electric","Ford Motor Company","Standard Oil","Goodyear Tire",2],["What fact about smoke did Morgan use in his design?","Smoke rises while cleaner air stays low","Smoke sinks while cleaner air rises","Smoke spreads evenly through a room","Smoke always moves away from heat",3],["Early traffic signals had only what two settings?","Stop and go","Slow and fast","Left and right","Day and night",3],["How did Morgan protect his ideas?","With patents","By keeping them secret","By writing a book","By giving away plans",4],["His safety hood is seen as a step toward which modern device?","The gas mask","The smoke alarm","The fire extinguisher","The oxygen tank",4]]}]},{"id":"jemison","name":"Dr. Mae Jemison","years":"born 1956","era":"current","tagline":"Doctor, engineer, and the first African American woman to travel into space","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Mae Jemison: Reaching for the Stars","paras":["Mae Jemison was born on October 17, 1956, in Decatur, Alabama, and grew up in Chicago, Illinois. Her parents cared about learning, and she read science books from the library. She also loved to dance and started ballet at age eight. She graduated from high school at 16 and went to Stanford University.","In 1992, Jemison became the first African American woman to travel into space. She flew aboard the space shuttle Endeavour as a mission specialist on mission STS-47. She orbited Earth 127 times and helped with science experiments. Before NASA, she worked as a medical doctor.","Jemison earned her medical degree from Cornell in 1981 and served in the Peace Corps from 1983 to 1985. She applied to NASA in 1985, but the Challenger explosion in 1986 paused the program. She applied again and was chosen in 1987. Her shuttle flight lasted from September 12 to September 20, 1992.","Mae loved science from a young age, and her parents supported her. In 1983, Sally Ride flew in space, and that flight inspired Jemison to apply to NASA. Jemison also wanted to help people, so she served in the Peace Corps as a medical officer in West Africa. Her flight showed many students that space was open to people like them.","Jemison built her path one step at a time, with degrees from Stanford and Cornell. When NASA paused after Challenger, she applied again, and she was one of only 15 chosen from about 2,000 applicants. Later she started a science camp for students called The Earth We Share. For an athlete, the lesson is not to let one setback end your goal."],"remember":"Jemison kept trying after a setback and became an astronaut.","quiz":[["Where did Mae Jemison grow up?","Chicago, Illinois","Decatur, Alabama","Stanford, California","Atlanta, Georgia",0],["At what age did she start ballet?","Eight","Five","Ten","Twelve",0],["Which space shuttle did Jemison fly aboard?","Endeavour","Challenger","Columbia","Discovery",1],["How many times did she orbit Earth?","127","87","157","210",1],["In what year was Jemison chosen by NASA?","1987","1985","1986","1992",2],["During which years did she serve in the Peace Corps?","1983 to 1985","1981 to 1983","1985 to 1987","1987 to 1989",2],["Whose 1983 flight inspired Jemison to apply to NASA?","Sally Ride","Neil Armstrong","John Glenn","Alan Shepard",3],["Where did Jemison serve as a medical officer?","West Africa","South America","Southeast Asia","Central America",3],["How many people were picked out of about 2,000 applicants?","15","5","50","150",4],["What is the name of the science camp she started?","The Earth We Share","The Stars Above Us","The Future Is Ours","Space Camp for All",4]]}]},{"id":"mandela","name":"Nelson Mandela","years":"1918-2013","era":"past","tagline":"Spent 27 years in prison, then led South Africa out of apartheid","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Nelson Mandela: From Prison to President","paras":["Nelson Mandela was born on July 18, 1918, in the village of Mvezo, South Africa. His birth name was Rolihlahla, and a teacher later gave him the name Nelson. He belonged to the Thembu people, and his family had royal roots. As a boy, he loved sports, especially long-distance running and boxing.","Mandela became a lawyer and a leader of the African National Congress, or ANC. He fought apartheid, a system of laws that forced people of different races to live apart and have unequal rights. After 27 years in prison, he helped bring apartheid to an end. In 1994, he became the first Black president of South Africa.","Mandela was born in 1918. He was arrested in 1962, and on June 12, 1964, he was sentenced to life in prison. He was freed on February 11, 1990, after 27 years behind bars. He won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1993, became president on May 10, 1994, and died on December 5, 2013, at age 95.","Under apartheid, Black South Africans had few rights, had to carry pass books to move around, and most could not vote in national elections. For years, the ANC used peaceful protest. Then, on March 21, 1960, police killed 69 people at a Sharpeville protest, and the government banned the ANC. In 1961, Mandela helped start Umkhonto we Sizwe, a secret group that used sabotage.","Mandela stayed strong in prison by exercising each morning and by reading and studying. In 1985, he turned down an offer of freedom because it meant giving up the armed struggle. After his release, he talked with President F. W. de Klerk instead of seeking revenge, and they shared the Nobel Peace Prize. Takeaway for an athlete: daily training and patience can carry you through very hard times."],"remember":"Mandela survived 27 years in prison, then worked with his old enemies to end apartheid and lead South Africa.","quiz":[["What was Mandela's birth name?","Rolihlahla","Jongintaba","Dalindyebo","Springbok",0],["Which two sports did Mandela love as a boy?","Long-distance running and boxing","Soccer and swimming","Rugby and cricket","Tennis and wrestling",0],["What did Mandela become in 1994?","The first Black president of South Africa","The first king of the Thembu people","A judge on South Africa's highest court","The leader of the ANC Youth League",1],["What was apartheid?","Laws that forced people of different races to live apart","Laws that sent every child to the same school","Rules that banned sports teams from traveling","A trade deal between South Africa and its neighbors",1],["On what date was Mandela sentenced to life in prison?","June 12, 1964","March 21, 1960","February 11, 1990","May 10, 1994",2],["On what date did Mandela become president?","May 10, 1994","June 12, 1964","December 5, 2013","July 18, 1918",2],["How many people did police kill at the Sharpeville protest?","69","27","96","11",3],["What did Black South Africans have to carry to move around?","Pass books","Work tickets","Travel maps","School cards",3],["How did Mandela keep his body and mind strong in prison?","He exercised each morning and kept reading and studying","He played rugby with the guards","He wrote songs for other prisoners","He slept late and saved his energy",4],["Who did Mandela talk with after his release instead of seeking revenge?","President F. W. de Klerk","Oliver Tambo","Walter Sisulu","Desmond Tutu",4]]}]},{"id":"drew","name":"Dr. Charles Drew","years":"1904-1950","era":"past","tagline":"Four-sport athlete turned surgeon whose blood bank work saved lives in WWII","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Dr. Charles Drew and the Blood Bank","paras":["Charles Drew was born on June 3, 1904, in Washington, D.C. At Dunbar High School, he earned varsity letters in four sports. He went to Amherst College on an athletic scholarship and played football and ran track. Then he studied medicine at McGill University in Montreal, Canada, and graduated second in his class of 137 in 1933.","Drew became an expert on storing blood so it could be used later to save lives. Plasma is the liquid part of blood, and it lasts longer than whole blood, so it can be shipped farther. In 1940, he became medical director of the Blood for Britain program, which sent plasma to Britain. In 1941, he helped lead a Red Cross pilot program that became the model for a national blood donor service.","Charles Drew was born in 1904 and earned his college degree at Amherst in 1926. He finished medical school at McGill in 1933 and joined the faculty at Howard University in 1935. In June 1940, he earned his doctorate in medical science, and Blood for Britain began that August. In 1944, he won the Spingarn Medal, and on April 1, 1950, he died in a car accident in North Carolina.","Wounded soldiers can lose a lot of blood, and whole blood spoils fast. Plasma could be stored and sent far, so Drew's research saved lives. When the armed forces separated blood by the donor's race, Drew called the rule unscientific and insulting. He also trained young Black surgeons at Howard University, because many hospitals refused to train them.","Drew built his work on careful study, and his 1940 thesis, Banked Blood, explained how to preserve blood. He set uniform procedures so every hospital followed the same steps. At Howard University, he taught surgery, and his first residents passed their board exams in 1948. Takeaway for an athlete: the practice and teamwork you learn in sports can help you succeed at anything."],"remember":"Drew, a four-sport athlete, became a surgeon whose blood storage work helped save lives and train others.","quiz":[["How many sports did Drew earn varsity letters in at Dunbar High School?","Four","Two","Three","Five",0],["How did Drew finish in his McGill medical school class of 137?","He graduated second","He graduated first","He graduated tenth","He graduated fortieth",0],["What is plasma?","The liquid part of blood","A kind of vitamin","A tool for surgery","A type of bandage",1],["What was Drew's job in the Blood for Britain program?","Medical director","Hospital driver","Army general","Red Cross nurse",1],["In what year did Drew join the faculty at Howard University?","1935","1926","1933","1944",2],["On what date did Drew die?","April 1, 1950","June 3, 1904","August 1, 1940","March 1, 1944",2],["Why was plasma more useful than whole blood?","It could be stored and sent far","It could be made without donors","It healed wounds on its own","It cost much less to produce",3],["What did Drew call the rule that separated blood by the donor's race?","Unscientific and insulting","Fair but too costly","Helpful but too slow","A needed wartime step",3],["What was the title of Drew's 1940 thesis?","Banked Blood","Safe Plasma","Blood Chemistry","The Blood Drive",4],["In what year did Drew's first surgery residents pass their board exams?","1948","1944","1950","1940",4]]}]},{"id":"walker","name":"Madam C.J. Walker","years":"1867-1919","era":"past","tagline":"Orphaned at seven, she built a hair care business and gave back to her community","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Madam C.J. Walker Builds a Business","paras":["Madam C.J. Walker was born Sarah Breedlove on December 23, 1867, near Delta, Louisiana. Her parents had been enslaved, and she was the first person in her family born free. Both of her parents died when she was seven, and she grew up poor. She worked as a washerwoman, washing other people's clothes for pay, and she raised her daughter, A'Lelia, after being widowed at about age 20.","Walker built a hair care business for Black women. She started in Denver, Colorado, with just $1.50 and sold her products door to door. She trained women as sales agents and organized them into clubs. In 1910, she moved her headquarters to Indianapolis and built a factory, and she became one of the most successful Black business owners of her time.","Sarah Breedlove was born on December 23, 1867, and was orphaned in 1874. Around 1904, she worked as a sales agent for Annie Malone. In 1905, she moved to Denver to start her own business, and in 1906, she married Charles Joseph Walker and became Madam C.J. Walker. She moved into her New York mansion, Villa Lewaro, in May 1918, and she died on May 25, 1919, at age 51.","In the early 1900s, Black women had few job choices, and many worked as washerwomen, cooks, or maids for low pay. Walker wanted a better life for her daughter and a better way for women to earn a living. Her business started with her own problem, because scalp trouble was making her lose her hair. By hiring women as sales agents, she gave them a way to earn their own money.","Walker started small, going door to door and showing women how her products worked. She built a team. Her daughter, A'Lelia, filled mail orders, and Freeman Ransom ran daily operations while Walker traveled. She also gave back, including $1,000 to a new Black YMCA in Indianapolis. Takeaway for an athlete: start small, build a team, and use your success to help others."],"remember":"Walker started with $1.50, built a team of women sales agents, and used her success to help others.","quiz":[["Where was Sarah Breedlove born?","Near Delta, Louisiana","Near Denver, Colorado","Near Indianapolis, Indiana","Near Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania",0],["How old was Sarah when both of her parents died?","Seven","Four","Twelve","Fifteen",0],["How much money did Walker start her business with?","$1.50","$15","$150","$1,500",1],["In 1910, where did Walker move her company's headquarters?","Indianapolis","Denver","Louisville","St. Louis",1],["In what year did Walker move to Denver to start her business?","1905","1904","1906","1918",2],["How old was Walker when she died?","51","41","61","71",2],["Which jobs did many Black women have in the early 1900s?","Washerwomen, cooks, or maids","Teachers, nurses, or lawyers","Bank clerks or store owners","Farm owners or factory managers",3],["What personal problem started Walker's business idea?","Scalp trouble that was making her lose hair","A bad back that kept her from work","A lost job at a laundry","A rash from washing clothes",3],["Who ran daily operations while Walker traveled?","Freeman Ransom","Annie Malone","Charles Joseph Walker","Mary McLeod Bethune",4],["How much did Walker give to the new Black YMCA in Indianapolis?","$1,000","$100","$500","$10,000",4]]}]},{"id":"owens","name":"Jesse Owens","years":"1913-1980","era":"past","tagline":"Won four gold medals at the 1936 Berlin Olympics and proved hate wrong","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Jesse Owens and the Berlin Games","paras":["Jesse Owens was born James Cleveland Owens on September 12, 1913, in Oakville, Alabama. He was the youngest of ten children in a family of sharecroppers. When he was nine, his family moved to Cleveland, Ohio, where a teacher heard his name as \"Jesse.\" There he met Coach Charles Riley, who let him train before school because he had to work after class.","Owens is best known for the 1936 Olympic Games in Berlin, Germany, where he won four gold medals in track and field. He won the 100 meters, the long jump, the 200 meters, and the 4 x 100 meter relay. A year earlier, on May 25, 1935, he had won four events in about 45 minutes at a Big Ten meet in Ann Arbor, Michigan. He set or tied world records that day.","Owens was born on September 12, 1913. He starred at the national high school championships in 1933 and began at Ohio State University that year. In Berlin, he won the 100 meters on August 3, the long jump on August 4, the 200 meters on August 5, and the relay on August 9. He died on March 31, 1980, in Tucson, Arizona, at age 66.","The 1936 Olympics were held in Nazi Germany, whose leaders wanted the Games to show that their hateful ideas about race were right. Those ideas were false. Owens won four gold medals in front of the whole world, which proved the claims wrong. Back home, he still faced segregation, and after a parade in his honor in New York, he had to ride a freight elevator to reach his own reception.","Owens built his speed with practice, and Coach Riley's early help gave him extra training time. At Ohio State, he trained under coach Larry Snyder and worked part-time jobs because he had no scholarship. He stayed focused even with a sore back in 1935 and with the world watching in Berlin. Takeaway for an athlete: find a good coach, use every bit of your time, and keep going when things feel unfair."],"remember":"Owens rose from a sharecropper's family to win four Olympic golds and show that talent has no color.","quiz":[["What was Jesse Owens's birth name?","James Cleveland Owens","John Charles Owens","Jesse Riley Owens","Jerome Clark Owens",0],["Why did Coach Riley let Owens train before school?","Owens had to work after class","The track closed in the afternoon","Owens wanted to avoid the team","Riley coached another team later",0],["How many gold medals did Owens win at the 1936 Olympics?","Four","Two","Three","Six",1],["About how long did it take Owens to win four events at the 1935 Big Ten meet?","About 45 minutes","About 15 minutes","About two hours","About one full day",1],["On what date did Owens win the 100 meters in Berlin?","August 3","August 4","August 5","August 9",2],["How old was Owens when he died?","66","56","76","86",2],["What did Nazi leaders want the 1936 Games to show?","That their ideas about race were right","That Germany had the largest stadium","That sports should stay out of politics","That the Games should be held every year",3],["How did Owens have to reach his reception in New York?","On a freight elevator","By using a side door","By climbing the kitchen stairs","By crossing the back garden",3],["Who was Owens's coach at Ohio State?","Larry Snyder","Charles Riley","Ralph Metcalfe","Frank Wykoff",4],["Why did Owens work part-time jobs at Ohio State?","He had no scholarship","His coach required it","He wanted to buy a car","His team had no funding",4]]}]},{"id":"king","name":"Dr. Martin Luther King Jr.","years":"1929-1968","era":"past","tagline":"Led the civil rights movement with peaceful protest and a dream of fairness","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Dr. King and Peaceful Protest","paras":["Martin Luther King Jr. was born on January 15, 1929, in Atlanta, Georgia. His father was a Baptist minister. King grew up in the South during segregation, a system of unfair laws that kept Black and white people apart. He started at Morehouse College at age 15, earned his PhD from Boston University in 1955, and became a pastor in Montgomery, Alabama, in 1954.","Dr. King became the best-known leader of the civil rights movement. He led with nonviolence, which means working for change without hurting anyone. During the Montgomery bus boycott, Black riders stopped riding the city buses for more than a year. On August 28, 1963, King gave his \"I Have a Dream\" speech, and in 1964 he won the Nobel Peace Prize.","The Montgomery bus boycott began on December 5, 1955, and in November 1956, the Supreme Court ruled that bus segregation was unconstitutional. On August 28, 1963, King spoke at the March on Washington. The Civil Rights Act became law in July 1964, and the Voting Rights Act followed in August 1965. King was killed on April 4, 1968, in Memphis, Tennessee, at age 39.","King lived when segregation was the law in much of the South. Black children often went to separate schools, and Black riders had to follow different rules on buses. As a minister, he believed his faith called him to treat all people with dignity, and he believed that fighting hate with hate would only create more hate. In 1959, he went to India to study the nonviolent methods of Mahatma Gandhi.","King's main tool was nonviolent protest. In the Montgomery bus boycott, thousands of people stopped riding the buses, and King taught them to stay calm even when others treated them badly. He was a powerful speaker, but he did not work alone. He helped lead the SCLC, a group that organized people across the South. Takeaway for an athlete: stay calm under pressure, trust your teammates, and show up again after a loss."],"remember":"Dr. King used peaceful protest, powerful words, and teamwork to help change unfair laws in America.","quiz":[["How old was King when he started at Morehouse College?","15","13","17","19",0],["What was segregation?","Unfair laws that kept Black and white people apart","A plan to build schools for every town","A rule that closed stores on Sundays","A program that helped families move north",0],["What did Black riders do during the Montgomery bus boycott?","They stopped riding the city buses","They filled every bus to the top","They walked out of their schools","They asked drivers for free rides",1],["What prize did King win in 1964?","The Nobel Peace Prize","The Pulitzer Prize","The Grammy Award","The Heisman Trophy",1],["On what date did the Montgomery bus boycott begin?","December 5, 1955","December 5, 1956","November 5, 1955","August 28, 1955",2],["In what month and year did the Voting Rights Act become law?","August 1965","July 1964","March 1965","April 1968",2],["What did King believe fighting hate with hate would do?","Create more hate","End the hate quickly","Make people braver","Change unfair laws faster",3],["Whose nonviolent methods did King study in India?","Mahatma Gandhi","Frederick Douglass","Abraham Lincoln","Booker T. Washington",3],["What did King teach people to do even when others treated them badly?","Stay calm","Walk away","Fight back","Stay silent",4],["What was the SCLC?","A group that organized people across the South","A college in Atlanta","A church in Montgomery","A law that ended segregation",4]]}]},{"id":"obama","name":"Barack Obama","years":"born 1961","era":"current","tagline":"Rose from a Hawaii childhood to become the first Black U.S. president","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Barack Obama: The 44th President","paras":["Barack Obama was born on August 4, 1961, in Honolulu, Hawaii. His mother, Ann Dunham, was from the United States, and his father, Barack Obama Sr., was from Kenya. As a boy, he lived in Jakarta, Indonesia, for about four years. He later attended Punahou School in Honolulu on a scholarship and graduated in 1979.","Barack Obama became the 44th president of the United States. He was the first Black president in the nation's history. He won the election on November 4, 2008, and took office on January 20, 2009. He won again in 2012 and served two terms, which is eight years, leaving office on January 20, 2017.","Obama graduated from Columbia University in 1983. From 1985 to 1988, he worked as a community organizer in Chicago. He earned his law degree from Harvard in 1991, and he married Michelle Robinson in 1992. He served in the Illinois Senate from 1997 to 2004 and in the U.S. Senate from 2005 to 2008.","Before 2009, no Black person had ever served as president of the United States. For much of the nation's history, Black Americans faced slavery, then segregation and unequal rights. Many people saw Obama's election as a major step in American history. At Harvard, he had also been elected the first Black president of the Harvard Law Review, a respected journal run by students.","Obama built his path one step at a time. He worked hard in school and graduated from Harvard Law School in 1991. As a community organizer in Chicago, he learned to listen to neighbors and bring people together, and he later wrote books, including \"Dreams from My Father.\" Takeaway for an athlete: big goals are built from many small steps, so keep improving every day."],"remember":"Obama grew up in Hawaii, worked step by step, and became the 44th president and the first Black president.","quiz":[["What country was Obama's father from?","Kenya","Indonesia","Canada","Ghana",0],["About how long did Obama live in Indonesia as a boy?","About four years","About one year","About eight years","About ten years",0],["What number president was Obama?","The 44th","The 43rd","The 45th","The 42nd",1],["How many terms did Obama serve as president?","Two terms","One term","Three terms","Four terms",1],["In what years did Obama serve in the U.S. Senate?","2005 to 2008","1997 to 2004","2009 to 2017","1985 to 1988",2],["In what year did Obama earn his law degree from Harvard?","1991","1983","1992","1997",2],["How did many people see Obama's election?","As a major step in American history","As a small change in voting rules","As a minor event in Hawaii's history","As a test of a new law",3],["Of which student-run journal was Obama the first Black president?","The Harvard Law Review","The Yale Law Journal","The Columbia Review","The Chicago Tribune",3],["What did Obama learn to do as a community organizer?","Listen to neighbors and bring people together","Run a large company","Coach a youth sports team","Write laws for the city",4],["According to the takeaway, how are big goals built?","From many small steps","From one lucky break","From a single big win","From natural talent alone",4]]}]},{"id":"tubman","name":"Harriet Tubman","years":"c. 1822-1913","era":"past","tagline":"Escaped slavery, then led dozens of others to freedom on the Underground Railroad","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"The Conductor Who Went Back","paras":["Harriet Tubman was born into slavery in Dorchester County, Maryland, around 1822. Her birth name was Araminta Ross. She later became known as Harriet and took the last name Tubman after marrying John Tubman, a free man. As a young person, she was hit in the head by a two-pound weight thrown by an overseer. The injury caused sleeping spells for the rest of her life.","In 1849, Tubman escaped alone and reached freedom in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania. She did not stay safe there. Between 1850 and 1860, she went back to Maryland's Eastern Shore 13 times. She guided more than 70 family members and friends to freedom along the Underground Railroad, a secret network of helpers and hiding places.","Tubman's enslaver died in March 1849, and she feared being sold farther south. She escaped that fall. In 1859, she bought a house in Auburn, New York. During the Civil War, she went to Port Royal, South Carolina, in 1862 and served the Union Army as a nurse, scout and spy. On June 1, 1863, she helped plan and lead the Combahee River Raid, which freed more than 750 people. She died on March 10, 1913.","Slavery treated people as property and tore families apart. Tubman feared being sold away from her own family. Escaping was dangerous because enslavers offered money rewards for captured runaways. The Fugitive Slave Act of 1850 made even the North unsafe for people who had escaped. Tubman went back anyway, again and again. Her courage proved that determined people could fight slavery, and she became one of the most honored figures in American history.","Tubman planned carefully. She traveled at night, followed the North Star, and relied on trusted helpers and hiding places. She knew the land, stayed calm when danger was close, and leaned on her faith. After the war, she settled in Auburn and helped others. A ship was named for her during World War II. Takeaway for an athlete: prepare hard, stay calm under pressure, and keep going when the plan gets difficult."],"remember":"Harriet Tubman escaped slavery, then risked her life 13 times to lead others to freedom.","quiz":[["What was Harriet Tubman's birth name?","Araminta Ross","Harriet Brodess","Araminta Tubman","Harriet Davis",0],["What did her head injury cause?","Sleeping spells","A lasting limp","Hearing loss","Daily headaches",0],["Which city did she reach when she escaped in 1849?","Philadelphia, Pennsylvania","Boston, Massachusetts","Auburn, New York","Richmond, Virginia",1],["How many times did she return to Maryland between 1850 and 1860?","13 times","5 times","40 times","19 times",1],["On what date did the Combahee River Raid take place?","June 1, 1863","June 1, 1861","March 10, 1863","July 4, 1862",2],["How many people did the Combahee River Raid free?","More than 750","About 70","More than 250","Over 1,500",2],["Which law made the North unsafe for people who escaped?","The Fugitive Slave Act of 1850","The Emancipation Proclamation","The Homestead Act of 1862","The Civil Rights Act of 1866",3],["What did enslavers offer to get runaways captured?","Money rewards","Free land","Paid jobs","Legal pardons",3],["What did Tubman follow while traveling at night?","The North Star","A paper map","Church bells","A river",4],["What was named for her during World War II?","A ship","A bridge","A stadium","A hospital",4]]}]},{"id":"ali","name":"Muhammad Ali","years":"1942-2016","era":"past","tagline":"Olympic champion, three-time heavyweight king, and a man who stood by his beliefs","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"The Greatest Stands His Ground","paras":["Muhammad Ali was born Cassius Marcellus Clay Jr. on January 17, 1942, in Louisville, Kentucky. He started boxing at about age 12 after someone stole his bike, and a police officer named Joe Martin became his first trainer. Young Cassius was fast, funny, and very confident.","Ali won the Olympic gold medal in the light heavyweight division at the 1960 Games in Rome, at age 18. Four years later he shocked the world by beating champion Sonny Liston for the heavyweight title. Ali went on to win that title three different times. He finished his career with 56 wins and 5 losses.","Ali beat Liston on February 25, 1964, in Miami Beach, and soon after he announced his new name, Muhammad Ali. In April 1967 he refused to join the army, and his title was taken away. He could not fight for more than three years. On October 30, 1974, in Kinshasa, Zaire, he beat George Foreman to win the title back.","Ali said he refused the draft because of his religious beliefs and his opposition to the Vietnam War. He lost his title, his boxing license, and years of his best fighting time, and many people turned against him. In 1971 the Supreme Court ruled in his favor. His stand made him a symbol of courage for people around the world.","Ali trained hard and used speed and footwork rare for a heavyweight. Against Foreman he leaned on the ropes, let him punch himself tired, and won in the eighth round. In 1984 he was diagnosed with Parkinson's disease, and in 1996 he lit the torch at the Atlanta Olympics; he died on June 3, 2016, at age 74. Takeaway for an athlete: believe in yourself and stand for what is right, even when it costs you."],"remember":"Ali won big fights in the ring, but his courage to stand by his beliefs out of the ring made him a hero.","quiz":[["What was Ali's birth name?","Cassius Marcellus Clay Jr.","Marcellus Ali Clay Jr.","Cassius Louis Johnson Jr.","Joseph Marcellus Clay Jr.",0],["What led Ali to start boxing as a boy?","Someone stole his bike","His father was a boxer","He lost a school fight","A teacher saw him run",0],["Ali won 1960 Olympic gold in which division?","Light heavyweight","Welterweight","Middleweight","Lightweight",1],["How many losses did Ali have in his career?","5 losses","2 losses","9 losses","12 losses",1],["Where did Ali beat George Foreman?","Kinshasa, Zaire","Louisville, Kentucky","Miami Beach, Florida","Rome, Italy",2],["In what year did Ali refuse to join the army?","1967","1964","1971","1974",2],["Which war did Ali speak out against?","The Vietnam War","World War II","The Korean War","The Gulf War",3],["What did the Supreme Court do in 1971?","Ruled in his favor","Sent him to prison","Took away his title","Banned him from boxing",3],["Which disease was Ali diagnosed with in 1984?","Parkinson's disease","Diabetes","Heart disease","Lyme disease",4],["In which round did Ali beat Foreman?","The eighth round","The first round","The third round","The fifteenth round",4]]}]},{"id":"lewis","name":"John Lewis","years":"1940-2020","era":"past","tagline":"He took a beating on a bridge for the right to vote, and never struck back.","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"John Lewis: Good Trouble on the Bridge","paras":["John Lewis was born on February 21, 1940, near Troy, Alabama. His parents were sharecroppers, which means they farmed land that belonged to someone else. John worked on the farm as a boy and loved church and school. He later studied at the American Baptist Theological Seminary in Nashville and then at Fisk University.","John Lewis spent his life working for equal rights through peaceful protest. He was one of the 13 original Freedom Riders, who rode buses through the South to challenge segregation. He later led a group called the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee. After that, he served in the U.S. Congress for more than thirty years.","The Freedom Rides began in 1961. In 1963, Lewis became chairman of the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee. That August, at age 23, he spoke at the March on Washington. On March 7, 1965, he led marchers across a bridge in Selma, Alabama. The Voting Rights Act became law that August. Voters first elected him to Congress in 1986.","In much of the South, Black citizens were blocked from voting by unfair rules. Lewis wanted to change that without using violence. On March 7, 1965, called Bloody Sunday, state troopers attacked the marchers, and Lewis suffered a fractured skull. Many Americans saw the news reports, and the outrage helped push Congress to pass the Voting Rights Act of 1965.","Lewis trained in nonviolence and stayed calm under pressure, even when he was hurt. He believed in getting into what he called \"good trouble, necessary trouble,\" meaning peaceful action against unfair rules. In 2011, President Barack Obama gave him the Presidential Medal of Freedom. He died on July 17, 2020. Takeaway for an athlete: staying calm and disciplined under pressure keeps you going when things get hard."],"remember":"John Lewis faced danger with calm courage and helped win the right to vote for millions.","quiz":[["What kind of work did John Lewis's parents do?","Sharecroppers","Teachers","Ministers","Factory workers",0],["In which state was John Lewis born?","Alabama","Georgia","Mississippi","Tennessee",0],["How many original Freedom Riders were there?","13","7","21","40",1],["Which group did Lewis later lead?","Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee","Southern Christian Leadership Conference","National Urban League","Congress of Racial Equality",1],["How old was Lewis when he spoke at the March on Washington?","23","18","31","40",2],["In what year did voters first elect Lewis to Congress?","1986","1976","1996","2006",2],["What happened to Lewis on Bloody Sunday?","He suffered a fractured skull","He was thrown in jail","He was forced to leave Alabama","He lost his voice",3],["Which law did the attack help push Congress to pass?","Voting Rights Act of 1965","Civil Rights Act of 1964","Fair Housing Act of 1968","Emancipation Proclamation",3],["What phrase did Lewis use for peaceful action against unfair rules?","Good trouble, necessary trouble","Quiet courage, steady courage","Stand up, speak up","Peaceful march, patient march",4],["Which president gave Lewis the Presidential Medal of Freedom?","Barack Obama","Bill Clinton","George W. Bush","Jimmy Carter",4]]}]},{"id":"morrison","name":"Toni Morrison","years":"1931-2019","era":"past","tagline":"Novelist and editor who won the Nobel Prize for telling Black stories","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Toni Morrison: Stories That Changed Books","paras":["Toni Morrison was born Chloe Wofford on February 18, 1931, in Lorain, Ohio. Her family was working class, and her parents loved stories and music. She read constantly as a girl. She later studied at Howard University in Washington, D.C., and earned a master's degree from Cornell University.","Morrison became one of America's greatest novelists. Her books include The Bluest Eye, Song of Solomon, and Beloved, which won the Pulitzer Prize for fiction. In 1993, she won the Nobel Prize in Literature, and she was the first Black woman to win that honor. Her books are rich, poetic, and deeply human.","Morrison worked as an editor at Random House for about twenty years, until 1983. She published her first novel, The Bluest Eye, in 1970, while working and raising two sons. Beloved came out in 1987, and she received the Presidential Medal of Freedom in 2012. She died on August 5, 2019, at age 88.","Morrison wanted to write stories for Black readers, about Black lives. Too many books at the time left those stories out. Her novels looked honestly at slavery and its long shadow, and at love, family, and community. She helped readers around the world see American history through Black eyes.","Morrison worked with great care on every sentence. She often woke before dawn to write, even when she had a full-time job. As an editor, she helped publish books by other Black writers, and later she taught at Princeton University. Takeaway for an athlete: steady work done early, day after day, builds greatness."],"remember":"Toni Morrison wrote honest novels about Black life and became the first Black woman to win the Nobel Prize in Literature.","quiz":[["In which Ohio city was Toni Morrison born?","Lorain, Ohio","Cleveland, Ohio","Toledo, Ohio","Dayton, Ohio",0],["Which university in Washington, D.C., did she attend?","Howard University","Spelman College","Georgetown University","Hampton University",0],["Which of her novels won the Pulitzer Prize?","Beloved","Song of Solomon","The Bluest Eye","Sula",1],["What prize did Morrison win in 1993?","Nobel Prize in Literature","Pulitzer Prize for fiction","National Book Award","Newbery Medal",1],["In what year did Beloved come out?","1987","1977","1993","2003",2],["What honor did she receive in 2012?","Presidential Medal of Freedom","Congressional Gold Medal","Kennedy Center Honor","National Medal of Arts",2],["What kind of stories were missing from many books of her day?","Stories about Black lives","Stories about sports heroes","Stories about the Wild West","Stories about space travel",3],["What hard part of history did her novels look at honestly?","Slavery and its long shadow","The Gold Rush years","The Revolutionary War","The Great Depression",3],["When did Morrison often write?","Before dawn","After midnight","During lunch breaks","On weekends only",4],["At which university did she later teach?","Princeton University","Harvard University","Yale University","Stanford University",4]]}]},{"id":"parks","name":"Rosa Parks","years":"1913-2005","era":"past","tagline":"Her calm refusal to give up a bus seat helped spark the Montgomery bus boycott","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"The Seat That Started a Boycott","paras":["Rosa Parks was born Rosa Louise McCauley on February 4, 1913, in Tuskegee, Alabama. She grew up in the segregated South, where laws kept Black and white people apart. As an adult, she worked as a seamstress in Montgomery and married Raymond Parks. In 1943, she joined the Montgomery chapter of the NAACP and was elected its secretary.","On December 1, 1955, in Montgomery, Alabama, Parks refused to give up her bus seat to a white passenger. She was arrested and fined $10 plus $4 in court fees. Her calm refusal helped spark the Montgomery bus boycott. For 381 days, thousands of Black residents stayed off the city buses. They walked, carpooled and found other ways to get to work.","Parks joined the NAACP in 1943. Her arrest came on December 1, 1955. On December 5, the day of her trial, the boycott began. It ended on December 20, 1956, after the Supreme Court ordered Montgomery's buses integrated. In August 1957, she moved to Detroit, Michigan. She died there on October 24, 2005, at age 92.","Montgomery's buses were segregated. Black riders paid the same fare as everyone else, but they had to sit in the back and give up seats when white riders wanted them. Parks had worked for fairness for years. The boycott showed that organized, peaceful action could change unfair rules. It also brought a young minister named Martin Luther King Jr. national attention.","Parks was quiet, but she was determined and prepared. Her years of NAACP work taught her how to organize. Many others helped, too. A federal court case called Browder v. Gayle, brought by other plaintiffs, ruled bus segregation unconstitutional. In 1999, Congress gave Parks the Congressional Gold Medal. Takeaway for an athlete: you do not have to be loud to be strong, because steady courage can change a game."],"remember":"Rosa Parks' steady courage, backed by years of organizing, helped end bus segregation in Montgomery.","quiz":[["Where was Rosa Parks born?","Tuskegee, Alabama","Selma, Alabama","Birmingham, Alabama","Atlanta, Georgia",0],["What office did she hold in the Montgomery NAACP?","Secretary","President","Treasurer","Lawyer",0],["How much was her fine, not counting court fees?","$10","$50","$25","$100",1],["How long did the Montgomery bus boycott last?","381 days","100 days","180 days","540 days",1],["On what date did the boycott begin?","December 5, 1955","December 20, 1955","December 1, 1956","January 5, 1956",2],["Where did Parks move in August 1957?","Detroit, Michigan","Chicago, Illinois","Atlanta, Georgia","New York City",2],["Where did Black riders have to sit on Montgomery buses?","In the back","In the front","Near the driver","On the left side",3],["Which young minister gained national attention from the boycott?","Martin Luther King Jr.","Medgar Evers","Jesse Owens","Thurgood Marshall",3],["Which federal court case ruled bus segregation unconstitutional?","Browder v. Gayle","Brown v. Board","Plessy v. Ferguson","Marbury v. Madison",4],["What did Congress give Parks in 1999?","The Congressional Gold Medal","The Medal of Honor","The Nobel Peace Prize","The Heisman Trophy",4]]}]},{"id":"jordan","name":"Michael Jordan","years":"born 1963","era":"current","tagline":"Six NBA titles, five MVP awards, and the most famous work ethic in sports","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Cut, Then Unstoppable","paras":["Michael Jordan was born on February 17, 1963, in Brooklyn, New York. His family moved to Wilmington, North Carolina, when he was five. He played baseball and basketball as a boy. As a sophomore at Laney High School, he did not make the varsity basketball team.","Jordan led the Chicago Bulls to six NBA championships. He was named Finals MVP all six times and won five regular-season MVP awards. He also won 10 scoring titles, and he averaged 30.1 points per game for his career. He won two Olympic gold medals as well.","Jordan hit the game-winning shot for North Carolina in the 1982 national title game. The Bulls picked him third in the 1984 draft. The Bulls won their first three titles in 1991, 1992, and 1993. He tried pro baseball in 1994, came back to basketball in 1995, and won three more titles from 1996 to 1998.","Jordan changed how the world saw basketball. His leaping dunks and clutch shots made him a global star, and fans in many countries learned the game by watching him. In 1992 he helped the \"Dream Team\" win Olympic gold. He showed that a player cut from a team can still become the best ever.","Jordan turned that high school setback into fuel and practiced harder than almost anyone. He built his game on defense, a strong mind, and a hate of losing. He entered the Hall of Fame in 2009, and in 2016 he received the Presidential Medal of Freedom. Takeaway for an athlete: use a disappointment as fuel, then outwork it."],"remember":"Michael Jordan turned being cut from a team into fuel, and he outworked everyone on his way to six titles.","quiz":[["Where was Jordan born?","Brooklyn, New York","Chicago, Illinois","Wilmington, North Carolina","Atlanta, Georgia",0],["What happened to Jordan as a sophomore at Laney High?","He did not make varsity","He broke his wrist","He won a state title","He moved to Chicago",0],["How many NBA championships did Jordan win with the Bulls?","Six","Three","Four","Eight",1],["How many scoring titles did Jordan win?","10","3","5","7",1],["Which team did Jordan win the 1982 title game for?","North Carolina","Duke","Georgetown","Kentucky",2],["In which year did Jordan try pro baseball?","1994","1982","1984","1998",2],["What 1992 team did Jordan help win Olympic gold?","The Dream Team","The Bulls","The Wizards","The Tar Heels",3],["What did Jordan show about being cut from a team?","You can still become the best","It means you should quit","It ends a career","It proves the coach is wrong",3],["In what year did Jordan enter the Hall of Fame?","2009","1998","2001","2016",4],["What honor did Jordan receive in 2016?","Presidential Medal of Freedom","Nobel Prize","Congressional Gold Medal","Heisman Trophy",4]]}]},{"id":"coleman","name":"Bessie Coleman","years":"1892-1926","era":"past","tagline":"Turned away by U.S. flight schools, she crossed an ocean to earn her wings.","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Bessie Coleman: First Black Woman Pilot","paras":["Bessie Coleman was born on January 26, 1892, in Atlanta, Texas. She was the tenth of 13 children. Her family were sharecroppers in Waxahachie, Texas, and she worked in cotton fields as a child. Later she moved to Chicago, where she worked as a manicurist at a barber shop.","Bessie Coleman became the first Black woman to earn a pilot's license. She also earned an international license. Back in the United States, she thrilled crowds with daring air shows. She hoped to open a flight school for Black Americans.","In November 1920, Bessie left for France to study flying. She earned her pilot's license on June 15, 1921. On September 3, 1922, she flew in her first big American air show on Long Island, New York. On April 30, 1926, she died in a plane crash in Jacksonville, Florida. She was 34 years old.","In 1920, American flight schools would not accept Black students or women. Bessie refused to give up her dream. Robert Abbott, who ran the Chicago Defender newspaper, urged her to study in France. A banker named Jesse Binga and the Defender helped pay for it. Her success showed that Black women could fly.","Bessie worked hard and saved money. She managed a chili parlor in Chicago and opened a beauty shop in Orlando to earn money for her own plane. She died before she could open her flight school. The U.S. Postal Service honored her with a stamp in 1995. Takeaway for an athlete: when one door is closed, find another way in and keep training."],"remember":"When U.S. schools said no, Bessie Coleman found another way and became a pilot.","quiz":[["In which Texas town did Bessie's family sharecrop?","Waxahachie","Galveston","San Antonio","El Paso",0],["What job did she do at a Chicago barber shop?","Manicurist","Barber","Shoe shiner","Cashier",0],["What did Bessie hope to open?","A flight school for Black Americans","A school for nurses","An airplane factory","A newspaper company",1],["What kind of license was she the first Black woman to earn?","A pilot's license","A driver's license","A nursing license","A boat captain's license",1],["On what date did she earn her pilot's license?","June 15, 1921","June 15, 1924","September 3, 1921","April 30, 1921",2],["In which city did Bessie die in a plane crash?","Jacksonville, Florida","Orlando, Florida","Dallas, Texas","Long Island, New York",2],["Who urged her to study flying in France?","Robert Abbott","Charles Lindbergh","Orville Wright","Booker T. Washington",3],["Why did she go to France to learn to fly?","American schools refused Black students and women","There were no airplanes in America","French pilots earned more money","She wanted to join the French army",3],["In which city did she open a beauty shop?","Orlando","Houston","Memphis","Miami",4],["In what year did the Postal Service honor her with a stamp?","1995","1975","1985","2005",4]]}]},{"id":"brown","name":"Jim Brown","years":"1936-2023","era":"past","tagline":"The NFL running back who ran for 12,312 yards, then used his voice for change","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Jim Brown: Power, Speed and a Strong Voice","paras":["Jim Brown was born on February 17, 1936, on St. Simons Island, Georgia. He grew up in Manhasset, New York. At Manhasset High School, he was a standout athlete in many sports. After that, he went to Syracuse University, where he became a college football and lacrosse star.","In 1957, the Cleveland Browns drafted Brown with the sixth pick. He was a huge runner who was also very fast. He led the NFL in rushing eight times. He won the league's Most Valuable Player award three times. He finished with 12,312 rushing yards, a record at that time.","Brown played for Cleveland from 1957 to 1965. In 1964, the Browns won the NFL championship. He retired in July 1966 at age 30, still a top star. He was voted into the Pro Football Hall of Fame in 1971. In 1988, he began a program to help people turn their lives around.","Brown knew his fame gave him power. In 1966, he started a group to help Black-owned businesses grow. In 1967, he met with boxer Muhammad Ali in Cleveland. Other Black stars joined him to support Ali's stand against the Vietnam War draft. Brown showed that athletes can speak up.","Brown was strong, quick and tough, and he was also one of the best lacrosse players ever. He later became a movie actor. His 1988 program, called Amer-I-Can, teaches life skills to gang members and prisoners. He died in 2023 at age 87. Takeaway for an athlete: your game ends someday, but the good you do with your name can last."],"remember":"Jim Brown was a legendary runner who used his fame to help others and speak up.","quiz":[["On which island was Jim Brown born?","St. Simons Island","Long Island","Staten Island","Hilton Head Island",0],["Which university did Brown attend?","Syracuse University","Ohio State University","Notre Dame","Michigan State",0],["How many times did Brown lead the NFL in rushing?","eight","three","five","eleven",1],["How many career rushing yards did Brown finish with?","12,312","10,508","14,264","9,876",1],["In what year did the Browns win the NFL championship with Brown?","1964","1957","1961","1966",2],["How old was Brown when he retired in July 1966?","30","25","35","28",2],["What kind of businesses did Brown's 1966 group aim to help?","Black-owned businesses","small family farms","sports equipment stores","new restaurants",3],["Which boxer did Brown meet with in Cleveland in 1967?","Muhammad Ali","Joe Louis","Sugar Ray Robinson","Joe Frazier",3],["Besides football, in which sport was Brown one of the best ever?","lacrosse","baseball","basketball","track",4],["What does Brown's Amer-I-Can program teach?","life skills","football drills","public speaking","job interviews",4]]}]},{"id":"truth","name":"Sojourner Truth","years":"c. 1797-1883","era":"past","tagline":"Escaped slavery and traveled the country speaking for freedom and women's rights","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"The Voice That Would Not Be Silenced","paras":["Sojourner Truth was born Isabella Baumfree around 1797 in Ulster County, New York. She was enslaved by Dutch-speaking owners, and her first language was Dutch. She was sold more than once as a child. She never learned to read or write, but she became one of the most powerful speakers of her time.","In 1826, Truth escaped slavery with her infant daughter Sophia. When she learned that her young son Peter had been illegally sold to Alabama, she took the case to court with help from friends. She won in 1828, and Peter came home. Later, she traveled the country calling for an end to slavery and for women's rights.","Truth was born around 1797. She freed herself in 1826, and New York's emancipation law took effect on July 4, 1827. In 1843, she changed her name to Sojourner Truth and began traveling to speak. Her book came out in 1850. In May 1851, she spoke at a women's rights convention in Akron, Ohio. She died on November 26, 1883, in Battle Creek, Michigan.","Truth spoke because she knew slavery firsthand. She wanted every enslaved person freed, and she believed women deserved equal rights too. Many people thought women should not speak in public, but she spoke anyway. Her Akron speech is often called Ain't I a Woman?, though the wording we know today comes from a version published in 1863 by Frances Gage.","Truth used her voice, her memory and her faith. She could not read, so she told her story to others, and it was published as a book. During the Civil War, she helped recruit Black soldiers for the Union Army. In 1864, she met President Abraham Lincoln in Washington, D.C. Takeaway for an athlete: your voice and your effort count even when nobody expected them."],"remember":"Sojourner Truth freed herself, won a court case for her son, and spoke out for freedom and equal rights.","quiz":[["What was Sojourner Truth's first language?","Dutch","French","German","Spanish",0],["What was her birth name?","Isabella Baumfree","Isabella Truth","Sojourner Baumfree","Isabella Van Wagenen",0],["What was the name of the infant daughter she took with her?","Sophia","Harriet","Frances","Elizabeth",1],["To which state had her son Peter been illegally sold?","Alabama","Georgia","Virginia","Louisiana",1],["In what year did she change her name to Sojourner Truth?","1843","1827","1851","1863",2],["In which city did she speak at the 1851 women's rights convention?","Akron, Ohio","Seneca Falls, New York","Boston, Massachusetts","Chicago, Illinois",2],["What is her Akron speech often called?","Ain't I a Woman?","I Have a Dream","A House Divided","Give Me Liberty",3],["Who published the version of her speech we know today?","Frances Gage","Susan B. Anthony","Harriet Beecher Stowe","Elizabeth Cady Stanton",3],["Whom did she meet in Washington, D.C. in 1864?","President Abraham Lincoln","Frederick Douglass","President Andrew Johnson","General Ulysses Grant",4],["How did she share her story without writing it herself?","She told it to others","She drew pictures","She sang it on stage","She used a printing press",4]]}]},{"id":"rudolph","name":"Wilma Rudolph","years":"1940-1994","era":"past","tagline":"Overcame polio to win three gold medals at the 1960 Rome Olympics","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"The Girl Who Learned to Fly","paras":["Wilma Rudolph was born on June 23, 1940, in St. Bethlehem, Tennessee. She was born early and was very small. She grew up in a large, loving family near Clarksville. As a young child she became very sick with scarlet fever, pneumonia, and polio, which weakened her left leg.","At the 1960 Olympics in Rome, Rudolph won three gold medals. She won the 100-meter dash, the 200-meter dash, and the 4x100-meter relay. She was the first American woman to win three track and field golds at one Olympics. Fans around the world loved watching her fast, smooth running.","Rudolph wore a leg brace for years as a child. At 16, she won a bronze medal in the relay at the 1956 Olympics in Melbourne, and she started at Tennessee State University in 1958. Then came Rome in 1960, where she became a star. She died on November 12, 1994, at age 54.","Segregation in the South meant Black families had few places to get good medical care. Rudolph's family took her to Nashville for treatment, and they massaged her leg every day, even though doctors doubted she would walk normally. Her success showed what courage and family support could do. She also became a role model for Black women in sports.","Rudolph worked hard, and her family helped with her treatment and kept her going when others doubted her. She joined her school track team in the eighth grade, and at Tennessee State she trained hard with coach Ed Temple. After sports she taught and coached young people and started a foundation to help children. Takeaway for an athlete: a setback is not the end, and patience and effort can take you farther than anyone expects."],"remember":"Wilma Rudolph went from a leg brace to three Olympic gold medals through family support and hard work.","quiz":[["Where was Wilma Rudolph born?","St. Bethlehem, Tennessee","Nashville, Tennessee","Melbourne, Australia","Memphis, Tennessee",0],["Which part of her body did polio weaken?","Her left leg","Her right leg","Her left arm","Her lower back",0],["How many gold medals did Rudolph win in Rome?","Three","One","Two","Five",1],["Which event did she win along with the 100 and 200 meters?","The 4x100-meter relay","The 400-meter dash","The long jump","The 80-meter hurdles",1],["How old was Rudolph when she won bronze in 1956?","16","12","19","22",2],["In what year did she start at Tennessee State?","1958","1956","1960","1962",2],["What did her family do every day for her leg?","They massaged it","They put it in ice","They had her swim","They took her to run",3],["Where did her family take her for treatment?","Nashville","Clarksville","Memphis","Atlanta",3],["What did Rudolph start to help children?","A foundation","A school","A restaurant","A sports league",4],["Who coached her at Tennessee State?","Ed Temple","Jesse Owens","Bill Russell","Joe Martin",4]]}]},{"id":"banneker","name":"Benjamin Banneker","years":"1731-1806","era":"past","tagline":"A self-taught farmer who built a wooden clock and charted the stars.","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Benjamin Banneker: Farmer, Stargazer, Scientist","paras":["Benjamin Banneker was born on November 9, 1731, on a farm near Baltimore, Maryland. He was born free, at a time when slavery was common in Maryland. He had little formal schooling, so he taught himself much of what he knew. Later he took over the family farm.","Banneker became a respected scientist and mathematician. He built a clock out of wood that kept accurate time. He studied the stars and correctly predicted a solar eclipse. He helped survey the land for the new capital, Washington, D.C. He also published an almanac, a yearly book of tide tables and star charts.","Banneker built his wooden clock when he was about 20. In 1789, he correctly predicted a solar eclipse. In 1791, he worked with Andrew Ellicott to survey the land for Washington, D.C. That August, he sent a letter to Thomas Jefferson. His first almanac came out in 1792. He died in 1806.","At the time, many people claimed that Black Americans were not as smart as white Americans. Banneker's work proved them wrong. In his 1791 letter, he asked Thomas Jefferson, then the secretary of state, to help improve life for African Americans and to work to end slavery. Jefferson replied and sent the almanac to a scholar in France.","Banneker taught himself astronomy and math. He studied the stars carefully and checked his numbers again and again. His almanac and his letter to Jefferson are remembered today, and a U.S. stamp honored him in 1980. Takeaway for an athlete: skill grows when you keep practicing carefully on your own, not just at team practice."],"remember":"Benjamin Banneker taught himself astronomy and math and proved his talent with careful work.","quiz":[["Near which city was Banneker born?","Baltimore","Philadelphia","Richmond","Boston",0],["How did Banneker learn much of what he knew?","He taught himself","A private tutor taught him","He trained in the army","He studied in Europe",0],["What was his clock made of?","Wood","Brass","Iron","Silver",1],["Which new capital city did he help survey?","Washington, D.C.","Philadelphia","New York City","Annapolis",1],["Who did Banneker work with on the 1791 survey?","Andrew Ellicott","Thomas Jefferson","James Madison","Benjamin Franklin",2],["In which year did he predict a solar eclipse?","1789","1769","1799","1779",2],["Who did Banneker write to, asking for help?","Thomas Jefferson","John Adams","Benjamin Franklin","Alexander Hamilton",3],["What did he ask Jefferson to work to end?","Slavery","Unfair taxes","Wars in Europe","Trade laws",3],["In which year did a U.S. stamp honor Banneker?","1980","1950","1960","1992",4],["What did Banneker teach himself?","Astronomy and math","Law and medicine","Music and art","Shipbuilding and banking",4]]}]},{"id":"hughes","name":"Langston Hughes","years":"1901-1967","era":"past","tagline":"Poet of Harlem who turned jazz, blues and everyday Black life into poetry","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"The Poet of Harlem","paras":["Langston Hughes was born on February 1, 1901, in Joplin, Missouri. His parents separated when he was young, and he was raised mostly by his grandmother in Lawrence, Kansas. She told him stories about Black history and pride. Later, he lived in Cleveland, Ohio, where he began writing poems in high school.","Hughes became one of the best-known poets in America. He wrote poems, stories, plays and newspaper columns about everyday Black life. His first book of poems, The Weary Blues, came out in 1926. He used the rhythms of jazz and blues in his writing, and he became a leading voice of the Harlem Renaissance, a flowering of Black art in New York City.","Hughes was born in 1901. In 1921, his poem The Negro Speaks of Rivers appeared in The Crisis magazine. That year, he started at Columbia University, but he left after about a year. His first book came out in 1926. He later graduated from Lincoln University in Pennsylvania. He died in New York City on May 22, 1967.","Hughes wanted to write about ordinary Black people, in their own voices, with pride. He wrote about work, music, dreams and hard times. In his famous poem Harlem, he asks, What happens to a dream deferred? That question spoke to people who were told to wait for equal rights. His poems gave Black readers pride and showed all readers a fuller picture of America.","Hughes listened closely to the people and sounds around him, from church to street corners to jazz clubs, and turned them into poems. He worked many jobs, including as a member of a ship's crew, to see the world. He also created a funny character named Simple for his newspaper columns. Takeaway for an athlete: pay attention to everything around you, because that is where great ideas come from."],"remember":"Langston Hughes turned the music and voices of everyday Black life into lasting poetry.","quiz":[["Where was Langston Hughes born?","Joplin, Missouri","Lawrence, Kansas","Cleveland, Ohio","New York City",0],["Who raised him for most of his childhood?","His grandmother","His uncle","His father","A teacher",0],["Which book was his first book of poems?","The Weary Blues","Montage of a Dream","The Crisis","Simple Stories",1],["Which kinds of rhythms did he use in his writing?","Jazz and blues","Marching bands","Opera and classical","Country and folk",1],["In which magazine did The Negro Speaks of Rivers appear?","The Crisis","The New Yorker","Ebony","Life",2],["Which university did he leave after about a year?","Columbia University","Harvard University","Howard University","Yale University",2],["Whose lives did Hughes want to write about?","Ordinary Black people","Famous athletes","Kings and queens","Business owners",3],["Which poem asks about a dream deferred?","Harlem","Mother to Son","Dreams","Theme for English B",3],["What character did he create for his newspaper columns?","Simple","Sample","Jesse Jones","Uncle Bob",4],["What kind of job helped him see the world?","A member of a ship's crew","A railroad porter","An army soldier","A school teacher",4]]}]},{"id":"payton","name":"Walter Payton","years":"c. 1953-1999","era":"past","tagline":"'Sweetness' ran for 16,726 yards and led the Bears to a Super Bowl title","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Walter Payton: The Heart of the Bears","paras":["Walter Payton was born on July 25 in Columbia, Mississippi. He grew up to be a fast, strong running back. He went to Jackson State University, a historically Black college in Mississippi. There he became a Little All-American. Teammates and fans gave him the nickname \"Sweetness.\"","The Chicago Bears drafted Payton in 1975 with the fourth pick. He played for the Bears for 13 seasons. He won the Most Valuable Player award in 1977. By the time he retired, he had run for 16,726 yards, the most in NFL history at that time. He also scored 125 touchdowns.","Payton ran for 275 yards in one game on November 20, 1977, against the Minnesota Vikings. On October 7, 1984, he passed Jim Brown's career rushing record. The Bears won Super Bowl XX after the 1985 season, beating New England 46-10. Payton retired after the 1987 season. He joined the Pro Football Hall of Fame in 1993. He died on November 1, 1999.","Payton showed that stars can come from small schools. After his death, the NFL named its top community service honor the Walter Payton Man of the Year Award. In his last months, he also spoke out for organ donation. He wanted people to sign up to help the sick. His voice helped more people give.","Payton was known for hard work and a team-first attitude. He started 170 games in a row, a record for a running back. His career rushing record lasted until Emmitt Smith broke it in 2002. Takeaway for an athlete: stars earn their place with effort in practice, not just talent on game day."],"remember":"Walter Payton combined hard work, toughness and kindness, and he helped others even at the end.","quiz":[["In which state was Walter Payton born?","Mississippi","Illinois","Alabama","Louisiana",0],["Which college did Payton attend?","Jackson State University","Grambling State University","Alabama State University","Howard University",0],["Which team drafted Payton in 1975?","Chicago Bears","Detroit Lions","Green Bay Packers","Cleveland Browns",1],["How many career rushing yards did Payton finish with?","16,726","12,312","14,264","18,355",1],["How many yards did he run in one game in 1977?","275","198","310","233",2],["Whose career rushing record did Payton pass in 1984?","Jim Brown","Gale Sayers","Emmitt Smith","O.J. Simpson",2],["What honor did the NFL name after Payton?","Man of the Year Award","Rookie of the Year Award","Comeback Player Award","Coach of the Year Award",3],["What cause did Payton speak out for in his last months?","organ donation","free school lunches","youth sports funding","safer football helmets",3],["How many games in a row did Payton start?","170","50","99","212",4],["Who broke Payton's career rushing record in 2002?","Emmitt Smith","Barry Sanders","Eric Dickerson","Curtis Martin",4]]}]},{"id":"bookertw","name":"Booker T. Washington","years":"1856-1915","era":"past","tagline":"Born enslaved, he built Tuskegee Institute and became a nationally famous educator","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"From Slavery to Tuskegee","paras":["Booker T. Washington was born into slavery in 1856 in Hale's Ford, Virginia. He was freed as a child when slavery ended after the Civil War. His family moved to West Virginia, where he worked hard jobs as a boy. He wanted to learn more than anything. That hunger for school drove nearly everything he did later.","At about age 16, Washington walked east to attend the Hampton Institute in Virginia. He paid his way by working as a janitor, and he graduated at 19. In 1881, he became the first leader of the Tuskegee Normal and Industrial Institute in Alabama. He built it into a respected school that taught trades, farming and teaching.","Washington was born in 1856 and graduated from Hampton at 19. Tuskegee opened on July 4, 1881. In 1895, he gave a famous speech at the Atlanta Exposition. His autobiography, Up from Slavery, came out in 1901. That October, he had dinner at the White House with President Theodore Roosevelt. Washington died on November 14, 1915, at age 59.","After slavery, most Black Southerners had little money or schooling and faced unfair laws. Washington believed education and job skills could build lasting independence. In his Atlanta speech, he urged Black and white Southerners to work together, but he did not demand full voting rights right away. W. E. B. Du Bois and others disagreed, saying Black Americans should push for full civil rights now. People still debate his views today.","Washington raised money from donors and hired strong teachers, including scientist George Washington Carver. Students at Tuskegee studied and learned trades at the same time. Julius Rosenwald, the head of Sears, Roebuck, joined Tuskegee's board in 1912 and helped fund rural schools for Black children. Takeaway for an athlete: build your skills one step at a time, because steady work creates lasting strength."],"remember":"Booker T. Washington rose from slavery to lead Tuskegee, and his ideas are still debated.","quiz":[["In what year was Booker T. Washington born?","1856","1861","1868","1881",0],["To which state did his family move?","West Virginia","Alabama","Georgia","Ohio",0],["How did he pay his way at Hampton?","Working as a janitor","Delivering mail","Painting houses","Coaching teams",1],["Which school did he lead beginning in 1881?","Tuskegee Institute in Alabama","Howard University in Washington","Morehouse College in Georgia","Fisk University in Tennessee",1],["On what date did Tuskegee open?","July 4, 1881","July 4, 1895","October 1, 1901","November 14, 1915",2],["What was the title of his 1901 autobiography?","Up from Slavery","Born to Be Free","The Road to Tuskegee","From Hampton to Freedom",2],["Who disagreed with his approach?","W. E. B. Du Bois","Frederick Douglass","Marcus Garvey","Thurgood Marshall",3],["What did Washington believe could build lasting independence?","Education and job skills","Moving to Northern cities","Winning elections","Starting newspapers",3],["Which scientist did he hire at Tuskegee?","George Washington Carver","Charles Drew","Garrett Morgan","Benjamin Banneker",4],["Julius Rosenwald was the head of which company?","Sears, Roebuck","Ford Motor Company","U.S. Steel","General Electric",4]]}]},{"id":"smalls","name":"Robert Smalls","years":"1839-1915","era":"past","tagline":"He sailed a Confederate ship to freedom, then served in Congress","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"The Daring Escape of the Planter","paras":["Robert Smalls was born on April 5, 1839, in Beaufort, South Carolina. He was born into slavery. At about age twelve, he was sent to Charleston to work. There he worked as a waiter, a lamplighter and a dockworker, and he learned the harbor very well.","In 1862, Smalls was a pilot on the Planter, a Confederate steamboat. Early on May 13, he took the ship out of Charleston harbor. Wearing a captain's hat, he gave the right signals at the forts. Then he steered toward the Union ships and raised a white flag. He delivered the Planter, with its cannons, to the Union.","Smalls was hired as a pilot on the Planter in March 1861. He made his escape on May 13, 1862, with 16 people aboard, including his wife and two children. Congress gave him a reward of $1,500. He was elected to Congress from South Carolina in 1874, and his last term ended in 1888. He died in 1915.","During the Civil War, the Union Navy was blocking Southern ports. Smalls wanted freedom for his family and for himself. The ship, its guns and his knowledge of Confederate defenses helped the Union. He later served as captain of the Planter and took part in 17 battles.","Smalls knew the harbor, the forts and the signals, and he planned carefully. He stayed calm, and his crew trusted him. After the war, he bought the house in Beaufort where he had been enslaved and lived there for the rest of his life. Takeaway for an athlete: study the field, then stay calm and trust your team when it counts."],"remember":"Robert Smalls used skill, planning and nerve to take the Planter to freedom.","quiz":[["Where was Robert Smalls born?","Beaufort, South Carolina","Savannah, Georgia","Richmond, Virginia","Norfolk, Virginia",0],["Which jobs did he do in Charleston?","Waiter, lamplighter and dockworker","Barber, baker and blacksmith","Teacher, tailor and farmer","Banker, printer and sailor",0],["What was the Planter?","A Confederate steamboat","A Union gunboat","A cargo train","A fishing schooner",1],["What did Smalls raise when he neared the Union ships?","A white flag","A red lantern","A blue banner","A ship's bell",1],["How many people were aboard the Planter during the escape?","16","8","25","40",2],["In what year was Smalls elected to Congress?","1874","1868","1880","1890",2],["What was the Union Navy doing to Southern ports?","Blocking them","Building forts in them","Training pilots in them","Selling goods to them",3],["What did Smalls want when he made his escape?","Freedom for his family and himself","A seat in Congress","A job on a Union ship","Money from the Navy",3],["What did Smalls buy after the war?","The house in Beaufort where he had been enslaved","A steamboat in Charleston","A farm in Georgia","A newspaper in New York",4],["What does the takeaway tell athletes to do when it counts?","Stay calm and trust your team","Change the plan at once","Work alone to win","Ignore the other side",4]]}]},{"id":"armstrong","name":"Louis Armstrong","years":"1901-1971","era":"past","tagline":"Trumpet genius whose solos and gravelly voice shaped all of jazz","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Louis Armstrong: The Sound of Jazz","paras":["Louis Armstrong was born on August 4, 1901, in New Orleans, Louisiana. His family was poor, and he grew up in a rough neighborhood. As a boy, he sang on street corners and worked odd jobs. At age 11, he was sent to the Colored Waifs' Home, where he learned to play the cornet.","Armstrong became one of jazz's first great soloists. His trumpet playing was bold, fast, and full of feeling. He also sang in a warm, gravelly voice and helped make scat singing famous, which means using wordless sounds as music. His records changed how musicians played jazz for generations.","In 1922, Armstrong moved to Chicago to join King Oliver's band, his hero and mentor. From 1925 to 1928, he made famous records with his Hot Five and Hot Seven groups. His song Hello, Dolly! hit number one in 1964. He died on July 6, 1971, in New York City.","In Armstrong's day, Black performers often faced segregation. Many hotels and clubs would not let them in. Still, his talent and joy won fans everywhere around the world. Jazz was born in Black communities, and Armstrong helped turn it into a gift that people of every background loved.","Armstrong kept practicing and performing for decades. He mixed hard work with a huge smile and a sense of humor, and he listened to other players before making his own sound. In 1967, he recorded What a Wonderful World, which is still loved today. Takeaway for an athlete: stay joyful, keep practicing, and find your own style."],"remember":"Louis Armstrong turned a poor New Orleans childhood into a lifetime of music that made jazz loved around the world.","quiz":[["In which city was Louis Armstrong born?","New Orleans, Louisiana","Chicago, Illinois","Memphis, Tennessee","Kansas City, Missouri",0],["Which instrument did he learn at the Colored Waifs' Home?","The cornet","The piano","The drums","The clarinet",0],["What is scat singing?","Using wordless sounds as music","Singing only in a high voice","Singing songs about trains","Singing without a band",1],["How did Armstrong sing?","In a warm, gravelly voice","In a high, smooth voice","In a soft whisper","In a loud opera style",1],["Whose band did Armstrong join in Chicago in 1922?","King Oliver's band","Duke Ellington's band","Fletcher Henderson's band","Jelly Roll Morton's band",2],["In what year did Hello, Dolly! hit number one?","1964","1953","1971","1988",2],["What did Black performers often face in Armstrong's day?","Segregation","Taxes on tickets","Bans on trumpets","Short contracts",3],["In what kind of communities was jazz born?","Black communities","Farm towns","Military bases","College campuses",3],["Which song did Armstrong record in 1967?","What a Wonderful World","Take the A Train","Blueberry Hill","Mood Indigo",4],["What did Armstrong mix with hard work?","A huge smile and a sense of humor","A strict, serious attitude","Long breaks from the stage","A fear of the spotlight",4]]}]},{"id":"ashe","name":"Arthur Ashe","years":"1943-1993","era":"past","tagline":"First Black man to win the U.S. Open and Wimbledon, and a voice for fairness","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Calm Under Pressure","paras":["Arthur Ashe was born on July 10, 1943, in Richmond, Virginia. His mother died when he was six, and his father raised him and his younger brother with strict rules. Ashe grew up in a segregated city. He was thin and quiet, but he loved tennis very much.","Ashe was the first Black man to win the U.S. Open singles title. He also won the Australian Open and Wimbledon, which gave him three Grand Slam singles titles. He was the first Black man chosen for the U.S. Davis Cup team, and later he helped the team win the Cup.","Ashe was picked for the Davis Cup team in 1963, the year he went to UCLA on a tennis scholarship. He won the U.S. Open in 1968, the Australian Open in 1970, and Wimbledon in 1975. In 1979 he had heart surgery, and he retired from tennis in 1980. He died on February 6, 1993, at age 49.","In Ashe's youth, many courts and tournaments in the South were closed to Black players, and he showed that Black players belonged at the top of tennis. Ashe also spoke out against apartheid in South Africa, a system of unfair racial rules. In 1992 he told the world he had HIV, which he got from a blood transfusion during heart surgery. He then worked to teach people about AIDS.","Dr. Robert Walter Johnson, a doctor in Lynchburg, Virginia, coached Ashe from about age 10 and taught him good manners as well as tennis. Ashe stayed calm and studied his opponents, and in 1975 he beat Jimmy Connors, who was favored, with smart changes of pace. The main stadium of the U.S. Open is named for him. Takeaway for an athlete: staying calm and thinking clearly can beat raw power."],"remember":"Arthur Ashe used a calm mind, hard work, and courage to break barriers in tennis and in life.","quiz":[["In which city was Ashe born?","Richmond, Virginia","Lynchburg, Virginia","Atlanta, Georgia","Baltimore, Maryland",0],["How old was Ashe when his mother died?","Six","Ten","Three","Sixteen",0],["Which team was Ashe the first Black man chosen for?","The U.S. Davis Cup team","The U.S. Olympic team","The U.S. Ryder Cup team","The U.S. Fed Cup team",1],["How many Grand Slam singles titles did Ashe win?","Three","One","Five","Seven",1],["In what year did Ashe win Wimbledon?","1975","1968","1970","1979",2],["How old was Ashe when he died?","49","39","57","62",2],["What system of racial rules did Ashe speak out against in South Africa?","Apartheid","Colonialism","Communism","Slavery",3],["How did Ashe get HIV?","From a blood transfusion","From a tennis injury","From a dental visit","From a trip abroad",3],["Who coached Ashe from about age 10?","Dr. Robert Walter Johnson","Pancho Gonzales","Stan Smith","Tom Okker",4],["Who did Ashe beat in the 1975 Wimbledon final?","Jimmy Connors","Bjorn Borg","Rod Laver","John McEnroe",4]]}]},{"id":"carver","name":"George Washington Carver","years":"c. 1864-1943","era":"past","tagline":"Born into slavery, he became a scientist who helped farmers heal worn-out soil.","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"George Washington Carver: Teacher of the Soil","paras":["George Washington Carver was born into slavery in Missouri, around 1864. He grew up on the farm of Moses and Susan Carver in Diamond Grove. In 1876, he left the farm to go to school. He worked odd jobs to pay for his education and kept moving to find better schools.","Carver became a famous scientist and teacher at the Tuskegee Institute in Alabama. He taught poor farmers how to restore worn-out soil. He found more than 300 uses for peanuts and many uses for sweet potatoes. He showed farmers how to rotate crops. Carver did not invent peanut butter, a popular myth.","Carver went to Simpson College in 1890. He then moved to Iowa State College, where he earned a bachelor's degree in 1894 and a master's degree in 1896. In October 1896, Booker T. Washington brought him to Tuskegee to lead agriculture. In 1906, he started the Jesup Wagon, a traveling classroom. He died on January 5, 1943.","Southern farmers had planted cotton year after year. Cotton drained nutrients from the soil, so crops grew poorly and many farmers stayed poor. Carver wanted to help them. He told farmers to plant peanuts, soybeans, and sweet potatoes in turns with cotton. These plants put nitrogen back in the ground, which improved the soil and the cotton crop.","Carver tested ideas in his lab, then took what he learned to farmers on the Jesup Wagon. He used his savings to start a research institute at Tuskegee. Later in 1943, a national monument honoring him opened in Missouri. Takeaway for an athlete: test ideas by trying them, and share what you learn to lift up your teammates."],"remember":"George Washington Carver turned science into lessons that helped poor farmers grow healthier crops.","quiz":[["In which state was Carver born?","Missouri","Alabama","Kansas","Georgia",0],["Whose farm did Carver grow up on?","Moses and Susan Carver","Booker T. and Fannie Washington","John and Mary Smith","Thomas and Sarah Jones",0],["At which school did Carver teach?","Tuskegee Institute","Howard University","Fisk University","Morehouse College",1],["What did Carver show farmers to do to help their soil?","Rotate crops","Burn their fields","Plant only cotton","Use much more water",1],["In what year did Carver earn his master's degree?","1896","1890","1906","1943",2],["What was the Jesup Wagon?","A traveling classroom","A farm delivery truck","A rail car for crops","A peanut-selling cart",2],["Which crop had Southern farmers planted year after year?","Cotton","Wheat","Corn","Tobacco",3],["What did peanuts and soybeans put back into the ground?","Nitrogen","Sugar","Salt","Iron",3],["What did Carver use his savings to start?","A research institute","A peanut factory","A farm in Missouri","A railroad school",4],["What opened in Missouri in 1943?","A national monument honoring him","A school named for him","A peanut museum","A new farm road",4]]}]},{"id":"dubois","name":"W.E.B. Du Bois","years":"1868-1963","era":"past","tagline":"Scholar who used facts and fierce writing to fight for full civil rights","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"The Scholar Who Demanded Equality","paras":["William Edward Burghardt Du Bois was born on February 23, 1868, in Great Barrington, Massachusetts. He grew up in a small New England town and was a top student. Teachers noticed his talent. He went south to attend Fisk University in Tennessee, where he earned a bachelor's degree in 1888.","In 1895, Du Bois became the first African American to earn a Ph.D. from Harvard University. He became a professor and a pioneering scholar who used facts to study Black life. His book The Philadelphia Negro came out in 1899, and The Souls of Black Folk followed in 1903. He also helped found the NAACP and edited its magazine, The Crisis.","Du Bois was born in 1868 and earned his Harvard degree in 1895. In 1905, he and other Black leaders met near Niagara Falls to launch the Niagara Movement, which demanded equal rights. The NAACP took shape in 1909 and 1910, with Du Bois as a founder. In November 1910, he began editing The Crisis. He died on August 27, 1963, at age 95.","Du Bois saw that after slavery, Black Americans still faced unfair laws and violence. He wrote that the great problem of the new century would be the color line, the way race divided people. Unlike Booker T. Washington, he said Black Americans should demand full civil rights and equal education right away. His ideas pushed the fight for equality forward.","Du Bois used research, writing and organizing. He trusted facts, believing honest research could break false ideas about Black Americans. Through The Crisis, he reached readers across the country. In his last years, he lived in Ghana, in Africa. He died there on the day before the March on Washington in 1963. Takeaway for an athlete: study the game, trust the facts, and let hard evidence guide your training."],"remember":"W. E. B. Du Bois used facts, writing and organizing to demand full equality.","quiz":[["Where was W. E. B. Du Bois born?","Great Barrington, Massachusetts","Atlanta, Georgia","Boston, Massachusetts","Philadelphia, Pennsylvania",0],["Which university in Tennessee did he attend?","Fisk University","Howard University","Morehouse College","Hampton Institute",0],["Which university gave him a Ph.D. in 1895?","Harvard University","Yale University","Columbia University","Fisk University",1],["What was the title of his 1903 book?","The Souls of Black Folk","The Philadelphia Negro","Up from Slavery","The Crisis",1],["In what year did leaders meet near Niagara Falls to launch the Niagara Movement?","1905","1895","1899","1913",2],["How old was Du Bois when he died?","95","79","84","101",2],["What did he call the great problem of the new century?","The color line","The wealth gap","The voting wall","The school gap",3],["Whose approach did Du Bois disagree with?","Booker T. Washington","Frederick Douglass","Marcus Garvey","Theodore Roosevelt",3],["In which country did he spend his last years?","Ghana","Kenya","Nigeria","Liberia",4],["His death came the day before which event?","The March on Washington","The Selma marches","The Montgomery boycott","The Brown v. Board ruling",4]]}]},{"id":"hamer","name":"Fannie Lou Hamer","years":"1917-1977","era":"past","tagline":"A sharecropper turned voting rights leader who asked America to do better","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Fannie Lou Hamer Speaks Up","paras":["Fannie Lou Hamer was born on October 6, 1917, in Montgomery County, Mississippi. She was the youngest of twenty children in a family of sharecroppers. Sharecroppers farmed land owned by someone else and gave part of their crop as rent. She grew up on a plantation in Sunflower County.","Hamer became a leader in the fight for Black Americans' right to vote. She joined the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee, called SNCC, as a field secretary. She helped organize the Mississippi Freedom Democratic Party. In 1964, she spoke to the Democratic Party's Credentials Committee about the violence activists faced.","In 1962, Hamer tried to register to vote, and she lost her job and her home. In 1963, she was jailed and beaten in Winona, Mississippi. On August 22, 1964, she gave her testimony at the Democratic Convention in Atlantic City, New Jersey. She died on March 14, 1977.","In Mississippi, unfair rules and threats kept many Black citizens from voting. The Freedom Democratic Party challenged the all-white state delegation. The party was offered only two at-large seats and turned the deal down. The effort helped push the nation toward the Voting Rights Act of 1965.","Hamer used courage and plain, powerful speech. She kept working for voting rights even after she was beaten. Her words reached people across the country. At the hearing, she said, \"I question America.\" Takeaway for an athlete: speak up for what is right, and keep going even when you are hurt."],"remember":"Fannie Lou Hamer risked everything to win Black Americans the right to vote.","quiz":[["Where was Fannie Lou Hamer born?","Montgomery County, Mississippi","Hinds County, Mississippi","Orleans Parish, Louisiana","Shelby County, Tennessee",0],["What did sharecroppers do?","Farmed land owned by someone else","Owned large farms and hired workers","Sold land to the state","Ran cotton mills in town",0],["Which group did Hamer join as a field secretary?","SNCC","NAACP","SCLC","The Black Cabinet",1],["Which party did she help organize?","The Mississippi Freedom Democratic Party","The Mississippi Farm Bureau","The Southern Voters Council","The Sunflower Citizens Club",1],["In what year did Hamer try to register to vote?","1962","1917","1964","1977",2],["In which city did she speak at the 1964 Democratic Convention?","Atlantic City, New Jersey","Chicago, Illinois","Philadelphia, Pennsylvania","Jackson, Mississippi",2],["How many at-large seats was the party offered?","Two","One","Four","Six",3],["Which law did the effort help push the nation toward?","The Voting Rights Act of 1965","The Emancipation Proclamation","The Homestead Act","The GI Bill",3],["What did Hamer do even after she was beaten?","Kept working for voting rights","Moved far away","Left the movement","Retired to a farm",4],["How does the lesson describe her speech?","Plain and powerful","Quiet and secret","Long and technical","Funny and light",4]]}]},{"id":"serena","name":"Serena Williams","years":"born 1981","era":"current","tagline":"Won 23 Grand Slam singles titles, the most in the Open Era","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"From Compton to Champion","paras":["Serena Williams was born on September 26, 1981, in Saginaw, Michigan. Her older sister is tennis star Venus Williams. The family moved to Compton, California, when the girls were young, and their parents, Richard Williams and Oracene Price, coached them. The sisters practiced together for years.","Serena won 23 Grand Slam singles titles, the most in the Open Era. She also won 14 Grand Slam doubles titles, all with Venus. She won four Olympic gold medals. In singles she won all four major titles and Olympic gold, which is called a Career Golden Slam.","Serena turned pro in October 1995 and won her first Grand Slam, the U.S. Open, in 1999. She won the Olympic singles gold in London in 2012. In January 2017 she won the Australian Open while pregnant, and her daughter was born that September. She stepped away from tennis after the 2022 U.S. Open.","Tennis was mostly seen as a country-club sport, and few Black players reached the top. Serena and Venus rose from Compton, not a place known for tennis champions, and they changed how powerful the game could be. Serena kept winning after becoming a mother, which inspired many women. She also spoke up for fair treatment of women athletes.","Serena trained for hours and was known for her powerful serve and mental toughness. Her sister Venus and her parents supported her along the way. She fought back after injuries and tough losses, and she stayed focused in big moments. Takeaway for an athlete: hard work and a calm mind in big moments can lift you to the top."],"remember":"Serena Williams combined power, hard work, and a fighter's mind to win 23 Grand Slam singles titles.","quiz":[["Where was Serena Williams born?","Saginaw, Michigan","Compton, California","Detroit, Michigan","Miami, Florida",0],["Who is Venus Williams?","Her older sister","Her mother","Her first coach","Her daughter",0],["How many Grand Slam singles titles did Serena win?","23","14","19","30",1],["How many Olympic gold medals did she win?","Four","One","Two","Seven",1],["Which major did she win while pregnant?","The Australian Open","Wimbledon","The French Open","The U.S. Open",2],["In what year did she win her first Grand Slam?","1999","1995","2012","2017",2],["What kind of sport was tennis mostly seen as?","A country-club sport","A school sport","A street sport","A team sport",3],["Which place did Serena and Venus rise from?","Compton","Houston","Miami","Chicago",3],["What was Serena known for?","Her powerful serve","Her slow pace","Her drop shots","Her net play",4],["What did she do after injuries and tough losses?","She fought back","She quit the sport","She changed sports","She took a year off",4]]}]},{"id":"latimer","name":"Lewis Latimer","years":"1848-1928","era":"past","tagline":"The draftsman who drew Bell's telephone and improved Edison's light bulb.","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Lewis Latimer: Lighting the Way","paras":["Lewis Latimer was born on September 4, 1848, in Chelsea, Massachusetts. His parents, George and Rebecca Latimer, had escaped slavery in Virginia. At age 16, Lewis joined the U.S. Navy during the Civil War. He served on the USS Massasoit and received an honorable discharge in 1865.","Latimer became a skilled draftsman, someone who makes exact technical drawings. He drew the patent plans for Alexander Graham Bell's telephone. He also invented a better way to make carbon filaments for light bulbs. His method helped keep the thin filaments from breaking. Later he worked for Thomas Edison's company and wrote a book about electric lighting.","After the Navy, Latimer got a job at a patent law firm. He started as an office boy and rose to head draftsman. In 1876, Bell hired him to draw the telephone patent. He applied for his filament patent in 1881, and it was issued in January 1882. In 1884, he joined the Edison Electric Light Company. His book came out in 1890. He died on December 11, 1928.","In the 1800s, electric light was new, and light bulbs burned out fast. Latimer's better filament helped make electric lighting more practical. His drawings helped Bell protect the telephone with a patent. Latimer was a Black inventor at a time of unfair treatment. His skill and hard work showed what Black inventors could do.","Latimer learned drafting on the job and kept getting better. He paid close attention to detail. Edison's company trusted him as an expert witness in patent court fights. His careful drawings made other people's big ideas real. Takeaway for an athlete: mastering the small details, like footwork and technique, makes you valuable to any team."],"remember":"Lewis Latimer's careful drawings and smart ideas helped bring the telephone and electric light to life.","quiz":[["In which state was Latimer born?","Massachusetts","New York","Ohio","Maryland",0],["How old was Latimer when he joined the Navy?","16","12","21","25",0],["What is a draftsman?","Someone who makes exact technical drawings","Someone who writes new laws","Someone who builds large machines","Someone who repairs street lamps",1],["Whose telephone patent plans did Latimer draw?","Alexander Graham Bell","Thomas Edison","Samuel Morse","Nikola Tesla",1],["In which year did Bell hire Latimer?","1876","1866","1886","1896",2],["Which company did Latimer join in 1884?","Edison Electric Light Company","Bell Telephone Company","Westinghouse Electric","General Electric",2],["What problem did light bulbs have in the 1800s?","They burned out fast","They were too bright","They made loud noises","They needed water",3],["What did Latimer's drawings help Bell do?","Protect the telephone with a patent","Sell telephones overseas","Build the first phone lines","Teach deaf students",3],["What role did Edison's company give Latimer in patent court fights?","Expert witness","Test pilot","Bookkeeper","Salesman",4],["How did Latimer learn drafting?","On the job","At a college in Europe","From a mail course","From a Navy captain",4]]}]},{"id":"aaron","name":"Hank Aaron","years":"1934-2021","era":"past","tagline":"Home run king who broke Babe Ruth's record with courage","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Hank Aaron: Hammerin' Hank","paras":["Henry Louis Aaron was born on February 5, 1934, in Mobile, Alabama. He grew up in the segregated South in a family without much money, but he loved baseball. In 1951, he signed with the Indianapolis Clowns, a team in the Negro American League. He later became known as Hammerin' Hank.","Aaron hit 755 home runs in his career, a record that stood for 31 years. He also finished with 3,771 hits and holds the record with 25 All-Star selections. He won the National League MVP award in 1957. Most of his career was with the Braves, first in Milwaukee and then in Atlanta.","Aaron made his Major League debut with the Milwaukee Braves on April 13, 1954. On April 8, 1974, in Atlanta, he hit home run number 715 off pitcher Al Downing of the Dodgers, passing Babe Ruth's old record of 714. He retired after the 1976 season and was inducted into the Hall of Fame in 1982. He died on January 22, 2021.","As Aaron chased Ruth's record, he received a flood of hate mail and threats. Some people did not want a Black player to pass a famous white star. Aaron refused to quit and kept playing, and many fans cheered him on. Later, he became a Braves executive.","Aaron was known for quick wrists and sharp eyes. He was not a flashy slugger who hit huge numbers in one season, but he was steady for 23 seasons. In 2002, he received the Presidential Medal of Freedom. Takeaway for an athlete: steady, daily work can beat one big flash."],"remember":"Hank Aaron broke Babe Ruth's home run record through steady work and courage under pressure.","quiz":[["Where was Hank Aaron born?","Mobile, Alabama","Atlanta, Georgia","Birmingham, Alabama","Milwaukee, Wisconsin",0],["Which team did he sign with in 1951?","The Indianapolis Clowns","The Kansas City Monarchs","The Birmingham Black Barons","The Homestead Grays",0],["How many home runs did Aaron hit in his career?","755","714","800","660",1],["Which award did he win in 1957?","National League MVP","Rookie of the Year","World Series MVP","Cy Young Award",1],["Who was the pitcher when he hit number 715?","Al Downing","Sandy Koufax","Don Drysdale","Tom Seaver",2],["In what year was Aaron inducted into the Hall of Fame?","1982","1979","1985","1990",2],["What did Aaron receive as he chased Ruth's record?","A flood of hate mail and threats","Offers from rival teams","Lawsuits from the league","Gifts from Ruth's family",3],["How did Aaron respond to the threats?","He refused to quit and kept playing","He took a long break","He asked the league to stop the chase","He left the Braves",3],["What was Aaron known for?","Quick wrists and sharp eyes","Huge arms and raw power","Great speed and bunting","A powerful fastball",4],["Which honor did he receive in 2002?","The Presidential Medal of Freedom","The Congressional Gold Medal","The Olympic Order","The Heisman Trophy",4]]}]},{"id":"bethune","name":"Mary McLeod Bethune","years":"1875-1955","era":"past","tagline":"Educator who built a college and advised a president","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Mary McLeod Bethune Builds a School","paras":["Mary McLeod Bethune was born on July 10, 1875, near Mayesville, South Carolina. She was the fifteenth of seventeen children. Her parents had been enslaved, and her family worked on a farm. As a girl, she wanted to learn, and she became one of the great educators of her time.","Bethune opened a school for Black girls in Daytona, Florida, in 1904. She started with six students, and one was her son Albert. Within two years, 250 girls were enrolled. The school later grew into Bethune-Cookman College. She also led national groups and advised President Franklin Roosevelt.","In 1904, Bethune opened her school in a rented house. Rent was $11 a month, and tuition was 50 cents. In the 1920s, it merged with the Cookman Institute, and it became Bethune-Cookman College in 1931. In 1935, she founded the National Council of Negro Women. She died on May 18, 1955.","In those days, many Black children, especially girls, had few schools to attend. Bethune believed education was the key to a better life. She also pushed leaders to treat Black Americans fairly. She used her White House access to form a group of Black advisers known as the Black Cabinet.","Bethune started small, with a rented house and big faith. She became friends with leaders, including First Lady Eleanor Roosevelt, and used those ties to help others. Her school became a college that still stands. Takeaway for an athlete: start with what you have, and keep building."],"remember":"Mary McLeod Bethune started with six students and built a college.","quiz":[["Where was Bethune born?","Near Mayesville, South Carolina","Near Daytona, Florida","Near Atlanta, Georgia","Near Memphis, Tennessee",0],["How many children were in her family?","Seventeen","Five","Nine","Twelve",0],["Where did Bethune open her school in 1904?","Daytona, Florida","Atlanta, Georgia","Charleston, South Carolina","Mobile, Alabama",1],["How many students did the school start with?","Six","Twenty-five","Sixty","One hundred",1],["In what year did she found the National Council of Negro Women?","1935","1904","1923","1955",2],["What was the school's tuition at the start?","50 cents","$1 a month","$5 a week","$11 a month",2],["What did Bethune believe was the key to a better life?","Education","Farmland","Sports","Travel",3],["What was the group of Black advisers called?","The Black Cabinet","The Freedom Council","The Women's Congress","The School Board",3],["What did Bethune start her school with?","A rented house","A new brick building","A gift of land","A church hall",4],["Which First Lady became her friend?","Eleanor Roosevelt","Lady Bird Johnson","Mamie Eisenhower","Edith Wilson",4]]}]},{"id":"dougwilliams","name":"Doug Williams","years":"born 1955","era":"current","tagline":"First Black quarterback to win a Super Bowl, and its MVP","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Doug Williams and Super Bowl XXII","paras":["Doug Williams was born in 1955 and grew up in Zachary, Louisiana. He became a quarterback at Grambling State University, a historically Black school. His coach there was the legendary Eddie Robinson. Williams had a strong arm. At Grambling, he threw for 8,441 yards and 93 touchdowns.","In Super Bowl XXII, Williams led Washington to a 42-10 win over the Denver Broncos. He threw for 340 yards. Washington trailed 10-0 after the first quarter. Then Williams threw four touchdown passes in the second quarter. He was named Super Bowl MVP. He was the first Black quarterback to win a Super Bowl.","In 1978, the Tampa Bay Buccaneers picked Williams in the first round. He played there for several seasons. In 1984 and 1985, he played in the USFL, a rival league. He joined Washington in 1986 as a backup. The Super Bowl was played in January 1988. He retired after the 1989 season.","For years, many teams doubted that Black players could play quarterback. Some moved them to other positions. Williams proved that doubt wrong on the biggest stage of all. His win helped open doors for many young players. Later Black quarterbacks, such as Patrick Mahomes, followed him to Super Bowl wins.","Williams stayed ready when he was a backup. Coach Joe Gibbs believed in him and signed him in 1986. In the Super Bowl, he hurt his knee in the first quarter but came back to play. Later, he coached at Grambling and won three straight SWAC titles. Takeaway for an athlete: be ready when your moment comes."],"remember":"Doug Williams stayed ready, took his chance, and showed that Black quarterbacks can win it all.","quiz":[["In which state did Doug Williams grow up?","Louisiana","Texas","Georgia","Florida",0],["Who was his coach at Grambling State?","Eddie Robinson","Joe Gibbs","Bill Walsh","Tom Landry",0],["What was the final score of Super Bowl XXII?","42-10","35-17","31-24","27-10",1],["How many touchdown passes did Williams throw in the second quarter?","four","two","six","three",1],["Which team picked Williams in the first round in 1978?","Tampa Bay Buccaneers","Washington","Denver Broncos","Los Angeles Rams",2],["Which league did Williams play in during 1984 and 1985?","USFL","CFL","AAFC","XFL",2],["What did many teams doubt about Black players for years?","that Black players could play quarterback","that Black players could run fast","that Black players could tackle well","that Black players could kick",3],["Which later quarterback followed Williams to a Super Bowl win?","Patrick Mahomes","Dan Marino","Joe Montana","Peyton Manning",3],["Which coach signed Williams in 1986?","Joe Gibbs","Eddie Robinson","Mike Ditka","Don Shula",4],["How many straight SWAC titles did Williams win as Grambling coach?","three","one","five","seven",4]]}]},{"id":"angelou","name":"Maya Angelou","years":"1928-2014","era":"past","tagline":"Poet, author and activist whose honest words gave millions of readers strength","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"A Voice That Rose","paras":["Maya Angelou was born Marguerite Annie Johnson on April 4, 1928, in St. Louis, Missouri. When she was about three, she and her brother were sent by train to live with their grandmother in Stamps, Arkansas. She grew up in the segregated South, where she found comfort in books and poetry. Later, she moved to California.","Angelou was a poet, author, singer, dancer, actress and activist. Her best-known book, I Know Why the Caged Bird Sings, came out in 1969. It was the first of her seven autobiographies. She also wrote poems such as Still I Rise. In 1993, she recited her poem On the Pulse of Morning at President Bill Clinton's first inauguration.","Angelou was born in 1928. At age 16, she became the first Black woman streetcar conductor in San Francisco. In the 1950s, she danced professionally. In 1960, she helped the Southern Christian Leadership Conference and was named its Northern Coordinator. Her famous book came out in 1969. She died on May 28, 2014, in Winston-Salem, North Carolina, at age 86.","Angelou wrote honestly about racism and hardship, and she also wrote about hope. Her work helped millions of readers see that pain does not have to defeat them. She was an activist in the civil rights movement, and she worked with Martin Luther King Jr. and Malcolm X. She showed that telling the truth about your life can give other people courage.","Angelou tried many careers, including dancer, singer, actress, writer and teacher, and she put her whole self into each one. In the early 1980s, she became a lifetime professor at Wake Forest University. Her recording of the inaugural poem won a Grammy Award. Takeaway for an athlete: a setback is not the end of your story, and you can build a new chapter."],"remember":"Maya Angelou turned hard experiences into books and poems that still give readers courage.","quiz":[["What was Maya Angelou's birth name?","Marguerite Annie Johnson","Maya Ann Jackson","Margaret Ann Johnson","Marguerite Anne Walker",0],["Where did she live with her grandmother?","Stamps, Arkansas","Memphis, Tennessee","Selma, Alabama","Tulsa, Oklahoma",0],["Which book was the first of her seven autobiographies?","I Know Why the Caged Bird Sings","Still I Rise","On the Pulse of Morning","Phenomenal Woman",1],["At whose first inauguration did she recite a poem?","Bill Clinton","Barack Obama","Ronald Reagan","John F. Kennedy",1],["What job did she hold at age 16?","Streetcar conductor","Radio announcer","Ticket seller","Waitress",2],["What was her title with the Southern Christian Leadership Conference?","Northern Coordinator","Southern Director","National Treasurer","Legal Advisor",2],["What did she write honestly about?","Racism and hardship","Sports and games","Space and science","Travel and food",3],["Which two leaders did she work with?","Martin Luther King Jr. and Malcolm X","Jesse Owens and Jackie Robinson","Thurgood Marshall and Medgar Evers","Barack Obama and Colin Powell",3],["At which university did she become a lifetime professor?","Wake Forest University","Harvard University","Howard University","Duke University",4],["What did her recording of the inaugural poem win?","A Grammy Award","An Oscar","A Tony Award","An Emmy Award",4]]}]},{"id":"biles","name":"Simone Biles","years":"born 1997","era":"current","tagline":"The most decorated gymnast ever, and a champion for putting health first","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Small Frame, Giant Skills","paras":["Simone Biles was born on March 14, 1997, in Columbus, Ohio. Her grandparents, Ron and Nellie Biles, raised her in Spring, Texas, and later adopted her. She started gymnastics at age six after a day-care field trip to a gym. She stands only 4 feet 8 inches tall.","Simone Biles is one of the greatest gymnasts of all time. She has won 11 Olympic medals, including seven golds. She has also won more world championship medals than any other gymnast, and several skills in gymnastics are named after her. She won Olympic medals at three different Games, from 2016 to 2024.","Biles won her first world all-around title in 2013, at age 16. At the 2016 Rio Olympics she won four golds and one bronze. At the Tokyo Games, held in 2021, she stepped back from most events to protect her mental health, then won a bronze on beam. At the 2024 Paris Games she won three golds and one silver.","When Biles arrived, gymnastics had few Black champions at the top, and she became the first Black woman to win the world all-around title. In Tokyo she showed that athletes can talk about mental health and put their safety first, and her choice made many people more open about it. She is a role model for young athletes in every sport.","Biles trains many hours each week with her coaches. She combines strength with very hard skills. After a long break, she came back and won again in Paris. Takeaway for an athlete: taking care of your mind is part of training, and coming back takes courage."],"remember":"Simone Biles pairs huge talent with courage, and she proved that taking care of your mind is part of being elite.","quiz":[["Where was Simone Biles born?","Columbus, Ohio","Spring, Texas","Houston, Texas","Cleveland, Ohio",0],["How old was she when she started gymnastics?","Six","Three","Nine","Twelve",0],["How many Olympic medals has she won?","11","7","9","14",1],["Which medals has she won more of than any other gymnast?","World championship medals","Floor exercise medals","Vault medals","Team medals",1],["How many golds did Biles win at the 2016 Rio Olympics?","Four","One","Two","Six",2],["What medal did she win on beam in Tokyo?","Bronze","Gold","Silver","No medal",2],["What did Biles become the first Black woman to win?","The world all-around title","The Olympic vault title","The U.S. junior title","The world floor title",3],["What did Tokyo show athletes can do?","Talk about mental health","Ignore all pain","Compete every event","Retire at a young age",3],["What does Biles combine?","Strength with very hard skills","Speed with long routines","Size with age","Luck with practice",4],["What does the takeaway call part of training?","Taking care of your mind","Resting for a month","Copying your rivals","Skipping practice",4]]}]},{"id":"woods","name":"Granville T. Woods","years":"1856-1910","era":"past","tagline":"Self-taught inventor whose telegraph let moving trains talk to stations.","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Granville T. Woods: The Black Edison","paras":["Granville T. Woods was born on April 23, 1856, in Columbus, Ohio. He had very little formal schooling. As a young man, he learned machinist and blacksmith skills in a machine shop. He taught himself about electricity and machines by reading and working hard.","Woods became a leading Black inventor of his time. His best-known invention let moving trains talk to train stations. It was called the Synchronous Multiplex Railway Telegraph. He also worked on electric railway systems, including a third rail. In all, he earned dozens of patents. People called him the Black Edison.","In 1884, Woods received his first patent, for an improved steam boiler furnace. He lived in Cincinnati, Ohio, at that time. In 1887, he invented the Synchronous Multiplex Railway Telegraph. American Bell Telephone bought the rights to one of his patents, which let him invent full time. He died in New York on January 30, 1910.","In the late 1800s, trains had no good way to talk to each other. Dispatchers could not easily tell where trains were, which led to crashes. Woods's system helped dispatchers and train crews stay in contact. It helped make train travel safer. He also showed that a Black inventor could succeed in a field where few Black Americans got a chance.","Woods studied on his own and kept learning. He had little schooling, but he used his machine-shop skills and tested his ideas. He protected his work with patents, so others could not simply copy it. His inventions helped shape electric railways. Takeaway for an athlete: a lack of formal training can be beaten by study and practice."],"remember":"Granville Woods taught himself electricity and invented ways to make trains safer.","quiz":[["Where was Woods born?","Columbus, Ohio","Chicago, Illinois","Detroit, Michigan","Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania",0],["Which skills did he learn in a machine shop?","Machinist and blacksmith","Carpenter and mason","Baker and tailor","Printer and sailor",0],["What did his best-known invention let moving trains do?","Talk to train stations","Run on solar power","Stop much faster","Carry more cargo",1],["What nickname did people give Woods?","The Black Edison","The Black Bell","The Black Morse","The Black Ford",1],["What was his first patent for, in 1884?","An improved steam boiler furnace","A telephone receiver","A railway brake","A light bulb filament",2],["Which company bought the rights to one of his patents?","American Bell Telephone","General Electric","Western Union","Pennsylvania Railroad",2],["What dangerous problem did his system help reduce?","Train crashes","Boiler fires","Track flooding","Late mail",3],["Who could use his system to stay in contact with train crews?","Dispatchers","Passengers","Farmers","Mail carriers",3],["How did Woods mostly learn?","He studied on his own","He went to a famous college","He worked as Edison's assistant","He joined the Navy",4],["What did Woods use to protect his work?","Patents","Secret codes","Special stamps","Private guards",4]]}]},{"id":"wells","name":"Ida B. Wells","years":"1862-1931","era":"past","tagline":"A journalist who used facts and courage to fight lynching","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Ida B. Wells Tells the Truth","paras":["Ida B. Wells was born on July 16, 1862, near Holly Springs, Mississippi. She was born into slavery and became free as a baby. After her parents died when she was a teenager, she worked as a teacher to help care for her younger brothers and sisters.","Wells became a powerful journalist. She co-owned a newspaper in Memphis and wrote pamphlets about lynching, which was when mobs killed people without a trial. She used facts to show the truth. She also fought for fair treatment on trains and for the right of women to vote, and she helped start the NAACP.","In 1884, Wells won a $500 court case against a railroad after she was forced from a train seat. A higher court later reversed it. In March 1892, a mob killed three of her friends in Memphis. Later that year she published Southern Horrors, and in 1895 she published A Red Record. She died in Chicago on March 25, 1931.","After her friends were killed, Wells set out to find the facts. She showed that many reasons given for lynching were false. A mob destroyed her newspaper office, and she never returned to Memphis. Her writing forced many Americans to face the truth, and it helped turn public opinion toward justice.","Wells did careful research and wrote with courage, even after she was threatened. In 1913, she helped start the Alpha Suffrage Club in Chicago. In 2020, she received a special Pulitzer Prize citation. Takeaway for an athlete: tell the truth, back it with facts, and keep going even when you stand alone."],"remember":"Ida B. Wells used facts and courage to make America face the truth about lynching.","quiz":[["Where was Ida B. Wells born?","Near Holly Springs, Mississippi","Memphis, Tennessee","Chicago, Illinois","Montgomery, Alabama",0],["What job did she take to help care for her siblings?","Teacher","Nurse","Seamstress","Store clerk",0],["What subject did her pamphlets cover?","Lynching","Railroad safety","Farming","Cooking",1],["In which city did she co-own a newspaper?","Memphis","Chicago","Boston","New York",1],["How much money did she win in her 1884 court case?","$500","$50","$5,000","$1,000",2],["In what year did she publish A Red Record?","1895","1884","1892","1913",2],["What happened to her newspaper office?","A mob destroyed it","It was sold to a rival","A flood ruined it","It moved to Chicago",3],["What did Wells do after her friends were killed?","Set out to find the facts","Gave up writing","Left to teach in Europe","Ran for governor",3],["Which club did she help start in 1913?","Alpha Suffrage Club","Free Speech League","Memphis Reading Circle","Women's Sports Union",4],["What honor did she receive in 2020?","A special Pulitzer Prize citation","A Nobel Peace Prize","A Medal of Honor","A Grammy Award",4]]}]},{"id":"ellington","name":"Duke Ellington","years":"1899-1974","era":"past","tagline":"Composer and bandleader who turned jazz into lasting American art","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Duke Ellington: Jazz Royalty","paras":["Edward Kennedy Ellington was born on April 29, 1899, in Washington, D.C. Friends gave him the nickname Duke because of his stylish manners and clothes. His parents taught him to carry himself with pride. His mother played the piano at home, and he started piano lessons at about age seven.","Ellington wrote hundreds of songs and longer pieces of music. He led his own big band, the Duke Ellington Orchestra, for about fifty years. His songs such as Mood Indigo became classics. Billy Strayhorn, his writing partner, composed Take the A Train for the band.","Ellington moved to New York in the early 1920s, and in 1927 his band began playing at the Cotton Club in Harlem. Radio made them famous. In 1956, a thrilling show at the Newport Jazz Festival brought him new fame. He received the Presidential Medal of Freedom in 1969, and he died on May 24, 1974.","In the 1920s and 1930s, jazz was often treated as simple dance music, and Black musicians faced segregation. Ellington proved that jazz was serious art. He wrote long works, including Black, Brown and Beige, about Black American history. He took his band to concert halls around the world.","Ellington wrote almost every day, often on trains and buses while the band toured. He wrote music for his players' strengths so each one could shine. He kept his band together for decades, even when touring was hard. Takeaway for an athlete: a team wins when each player's strengths are put to use."],"remember":"Duke Ellington proved jazz was serious art by writing music that let every member of his band shine.","quiz":[["In which city was Duke Ellington born?","Washington, D.C.","New York, New York","New Orleans, Louisiana","Chicago, Illinois",0],["What nickname did his friends give him?","Duke","Count","King","Prince",0],["For about how long did he lead his own big band?","About fifty years","About ten years","About twenty years","About thirty years",1],["Who composed Take the A Train for the band?","Billy Strayhorn","Louis Armstrong","Count Basie","Fletcher Henderson",1],["At which famous Harlem club did his band begin playing in 1927?","The Cotton Club","The Apollo Theater","The Savoy Ballroom","The Blue Note",2],["At which festival did he have a thrilling show in 1956?","The Newport Jazz Festival","The Monterey Jazz Festival","The Chicago Blues Festival","The Montreux Festival",2],["What did Ellington prove about jazz?","That jazz was serious art","That jazz was only for dancing","That jazz was a passing fad","That jazz needed no practice",3],["What was Black, Brown and Beige about?","Black American history","The history of baseball","Life on the railroads","The story of Harlem's clubs",3],["Where did Ellington often write music?","On trains and buses","At a desk at home","In a church balcony","Backstage at stadiums",4],["How did he get the best from his players?","He wrote music for his players' strengths","He made them play the same notes","He told them to copy other bands","He changed the lineup yearly",4]]}]},{"id":"malcolmx","name":"Malcolm X","years":"1925-1965","era":"past","tagline":"A powerful speaker for Black pride whose views grew and changed over his life","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Words, Pride and Change","paras":["Malcolm X was born Malcolm Little on May 19, 1925, in Omaha, Nebraska. His father, Earl Little, was a minister who spoke up for Black pride. His mother was named Louise. The family later settled in Michigan, where Malcolm grew up. He was a bright student, but life became hard for his family.","Malcolm X became one of the most influential Black leaders of the 1950s and 1960s. After time in prison as a young man, he joined the Nation of Islam and became its best-known spokesman. He spoke about Black pride, self-respect and demanding rights. Later, he left the group, traveled to Mecca, and began to speak more about unity among people of all races.","Malcolm was born in 1925. In 1946, he was sent to prison for burglary, and he was released in 1952. In March 1964, he publicly left the Nation of Islam. That spring, he traveled to Mecca for a religious pilgrimage. On February 21, 1965, he was shot and killed in New York City as he prepared to speak. He was 39 years old.","Malcolm X lived when Black Americans faced segregation, unequal schools and unfair treatment, in the North as well as the South. He told people to be proud of their history and to take charge of their own communities. His message of pride reached many young people. His views changed over time, which reminds us that learning never stops.","Malcolm X used words as his tool. He read a great deal in prison, practiced debating, and gave powerful speeches. He worked with writer Alex Haley on The Autobiography of Malcolm X, published in 1965 after his death, and it is still widely read. He showed he was willing to change his mind when he learned more. Takeaway for an athlete: keep learning, and be brave enough to grow."],"remember":"Malcolm X taught Black pride and self-respect, and he showed that people can learn and grow.","quiz":[["Where was Malcolm X born?","Omaha, Nebraska","Lansing, Michigan","New York City","Atlanta, Georgia",0],["What was his birth name?","Malcolm Little","Malcolm Carter","Malcolm Haley","Malcolm Evers",0],["Which group did he join and become a spokesman for?","The Nation of Islam","The NAACP","The Urban League","The Freedom Riders",1],["Which city did he travel to after leaving the group?","Mecca","Cairo","Jerusalem","Lagos",1],["When did he publicly leave the Nation of Islam?","March 1964","August 1952","April 1958","May 1962",2],["How old was he when he died?","39","29","45","52",2],["Where did unfair treatment exist, according to the lesson?","In the North and the South","Only in the South","Only in big cities","Only in schools",3],["What did he say Black people should take charge of?","Their own communities","The city governments","The public schools","The big banks",3],["Who helped him write his autobiography?","Alex Haley","James Baldwin","Ralph Ellison","Richard Wright",4],["What did he show he was willing to do?","Change his mind","Stay quiet","Avoid debates","Give up",4]]}]},{"id":"chisholm","name":"Shirley Chisholm","years":"1924-2005","era":"past","tagline":"First Black woman in Congress and first to seek a major party's presidential nomination","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Unbought and Unbossed","paras":["Shirley Chisholm was born on November 30, 1924, in Brooklyn, New York. When she was about five, she went to live with her grandmother in Barbados, where she went to school. She came back to Brooklyn in 1934. She later graduated from Brooklyn College and became an early childhood teacher.","In 1968 Chisholm became the first Black woman elected to the U.S. Congress. She represented Brooklyn's 12th district. In 1972 she ran for president as the first Black candidate to seek a major party's nomination and the first woman to seek the Democratic nomination. She served seven terms in Congress.","Chisholm was elected to the New York State Assembly in 1964. In 1968 she won a seat in Congress and took office in January 1969, and in 1971 she helped found the Congressional Black Caucus. On January 25, 1972, she announced her run for president. She left Congress in 1983 and died on January 1, 2005, at age 80.","In the 1960s, almost all members of Congress were white men. Chisholm said she belonged to no boss, and her slogan was \"Unbought and Unbossed.\" She fought for poor families, children, and schools, and she helped expand food stamps and create the WIC program for mothers and babies. Her run for president opened doors for later candidates.","Chisholm used her teaching background and her knowledge of children's needs to push for new laws. She spoke plainly and did not wait for permission. She won her 1968 election by a wide margin over civil rights leader James Farmer, and in 2015 President Barack Obama gave her the Presidential Medal of Freedom. Takeaway for an athlete: do not wait for permission to lead, and open the door for others."],"remember":"Shirley Chisholm broke barriers by speaking plainly and fighting for people others ignored.","quiz":[["Where was Chisholm born?","Brooklyn, New York","Barbados","Harlem, New York","Atlanta, Georgia",0],["Where did she go to live with her grandmother at about age five?","Barbados","Florida","Jamaica","Puerto Rico",0],["What was Chisholm the first Black woman to be elected to?","The U.S. Congress","The Supreme Court","The governor's office","The mayor's office",1],["How many terms did she serve in Congress?","Seven","Two","Four","Ten",1],["When did she announce her run for president?","January 25, 1972","January 25, 1968","January 25, 1976","January 1, 1983",2],["What did she help found in 1971?","The Congressional Black Caucus","The NAACP","The Urban League","The Peace Corps",2],["What was her campaign slogan?","Unbought and Unbossed","Fighting for Every Child","Power to the People","Ready to Lead Today",3],["Which program for mothers and babies did she help create?","The WIC program","The Head Start program","The Medicare program","The Job Corps program",3],["Who did Chisholm beat in the 1968 election?","James Farmer","Adam Clayton Powell Jr.","Jesse Jackson","Bayard Rustin",4],["What honor did President Obama give her in 2015?","The Presidential Medal of Freedom","The Congressional Gold Medal","The Nobel Peace Prize","The Medal of Honor",4]]}]},{"id":"dhwilliams","name":"Dr. Daniel Hale Williams","years":"1856-1931","era":"past","tagline":"Surgeon who founded Provident Hospital and repaired a wound near the heart.","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Dr. Daniel Hale Williams: Pioneer Surgeon","paras":["Daniel Hale Williams was born on January 18, 1856, in Hollidaysburg, Pennsylvania. His father died when Daniel was about nine. As a young man, Daniel worked as a barber and a shoemaker's helper. Then he decided to study medicine. He learned from a doctor named Henry Palmer in Wisconsin.","Dr. Williams became a leading Black surgeon. He founded Provident Hospital in Chicago, which welcomed patients of all races and trained Black nurses. In 1893, he repaired a stab wound close to a man's heart. The patient, James Cornish, lived. People remember it as an early, successful heart-area surgery.","In 1880, Williams entered Chicago Medical College. He earned his medical degree in 1883. He opened Provident Hospital in 1891. In July 1893, he operated on James Cornish. Later in the 1890s, he led Freedmen's Hospital in Washington, D.C. In 1913, he became a charter member of the American College of Surgeons. He died on August 4, 1931.","In the 1800s, many hospitals refused to train Black nurses or let Black doctors work. Many Black patients also got poor care. Williams wanted to change that. Provident Hospital gave Black doctors and nurses a place to train and work. It treated patients of every race.","Williams trained hard, worked with a team of doctors and nurses, and stayed calm when a life was at stake. Cornish left the hospital about fifty days later. A Chicago community leader, Mary Jane Richardson Jones, helped pay for Williams's education. Takeaway for an athlete: calm focus and teamwork help you perform when the pressure is highest."],"remember":"Daniel Hale Williams opened doors for Black doctors and nurses and made surgery history.","quiz":[["Where was Williams born?","Hollidaysburg, Pennsylvania","Chicago, Illinois","Madison, Wisconsin","Baltimore, Maryland",0],["Which jobs did Williams do before studying medicine?","Barber and shoemaker's helper","Farmer and miner","Teacher and sailor","Clerk and printer",0],["What hospital did Williams found in Chicago?","Provident Hospital","Cook County Hospital","Mercy General Hospital","Freedmen's Hospital",1],["What was the name of the patient with the stab wound?","James Cornish","Henry Palmer","Robert Abbott","Charles Brown",1],["In which year did Williams earn his medical degree?","1883","1873","1888","1898",2],["In which year did he become a charter member of the American College of Surgeons?","1913","1903","1923","1898",2],["What did many hospitals refuse to do in the 1800s?","Train Black nurses or let Black doctors work","Treat patients who could not pay","Allow visitors at night","Use new machines and tools",3],["Which patients did Provident Hospital treat?","Patients of every race","Only Black patients","Only soldiers","Only children",3],["Who helped pay for Williams's education?","Mary Jane Richardson Jones","Frederick Douglass","Julius Rosenwald","Jane Addams",4],["About how long until Cornish left the hospital?","About fifty days","About five days","About two weeks","About six months",4]]}]},{"id":"russell","name":"Bill Russell","years":"1934-2022","era":"past","tagline":"Celtics center who won 11 NBA titles and spoke out for equality","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Bill Russell: Champion and Leader","paras":["Bill Russell was born on February 12, 1934, in West Monroe, Louisiana. When he was a boy, his family moved to Oakland, California. He was not a star at first. He was tall and skinny, and he worked hard to learn the game at McClymonds High School.","Russell played for the Boston Celtics from 1956 to 1969. He won 11 NBA championships, more than any other player in league history. He was a five-time MVP and a 12-time All-Star, and he is famous for his defense, especially blocking shots and grabbing rebounds. He changed how people think about defense.","Russell led the University of San Francisco to NCAA titles in 1955 and 1956. In 1956, he captained the U.S. team to the Olympic gold medal in Melbourne. The Celtics won eight straight titles from 1959 to 1966. He received the Presidential Medal of Freedom in 2011, and he died on July 31, 2022.","In 1966, Russell became the first Black head coach in NBA history. He won titles as a player-coach in 1968 and 1969. Off the court, he spoke out against racism and unfair treatment, even when some fans did not like it. He also backed the civil rights movement.","Russell studied the game like a puzzle. He used timing, quickness, and smart positioning, not just height, to block shots. He and coach Red Auerbach built a team that valued passing and teamwork over individual stats. Takeaway for an athlete: the best players make their teammates better."],"remember":"Bill Russell won 11 titles by putting team first, and he used his voice to stand up for fairness.","quiz":[["Where was Bill Russell born?","West Monroe, Louisiana","Boston, Massachusetts","Memphis, Tennessee","Houston, Texas",0],["Where did his family move when he was a boy?","Oakland, California","Los Angeles, California","Seattle, Washington","Denver, Colorado",0],["How many NBA championships did Russell win?","11","7","9","13",1],["What was he famous for?","His defense","His three-point shooting","His fast dribbling","His free throws",1],["In which city did the Olympic gold medal game take place in 1956?","Melbourne","Rome","Tokyo","Helsinki",2],["How many straight titles did the Celtics win from 1959 to 1966?","Eight","Five","Six","Ten",2],["What first did Russell achieve in 1966?","First Black head coach in NBA history","First Black player in the NBA","First Black owner of an NBA team","First Black general manager",3],["In which years did he win titles as a player-coach?","1968 and 1969","1966 and 1967","1970 and 1971","1957 and 1958",3],["Who was the Celtics coach who built the team with him?","Red Auerbach","Phil Jackson","Bob Cousy","Wilt Chamberlain",4],["What did their team value over individual stats?","Passing and teamwork","Scoring and speed","Size and strength","Fame and money",4]]}]},{"id":"attucks","name":"Crispus Attucks","years":"c. 1723-1770","era":"past","tagline":"The first person killed in the Boston Massacre, a symbol of liberty","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Crispus Attucks and King Street","paras":["Not much is known for sure about Crispus Attucks. He was probably born around 1723. Many accounts say he was of African and Native American family. Some say he had once been enslaved near Framingham, Massachusetts, but that is not certain. He worked as a dockworker and sailor.","On March 5, 1770, a crowd gathered near British soldiers on King Street in Boston. The soldiers fired into the crowd. Attucks was the first person killed. Five men died in all, and the event became known as the Boston Massacre. Over time, Attucks became a symbol of the fight for liberty.","The Boston Massacre took place on March 5, 1770. The captain and his soldiers were arrested the next day. John Adams defended them at their trial later that year, and most were found not guilty. The five victims were buried together in the Granary Burying Ground in Boston.","Many people in Boston were angry about British soldiers in their city and about British rule. Leaders such as Samuel Adams and Paul Revere later retold the story to build support for liberty. In the 1800s, abolitionists remembered Attucks to show that Black people helped win American freedom. Historians still debate his exact role that night.","Few records survive, so we know little about his own words or plans. His story lasted because writers, artists and speakers kept telling it. Today he is honored as one of the first heroes of the American Revolution story. Takeaway for an athlete: you cannot control the headlines, but you can stand up for what you believe."],"remember":"Crispus Attucks was the first person killed in the Boston Massacre, and he became a symbol of liberty.","quiz":[["Around what year was Attucks probably born?","1723","1700","1745","1752",0],["What work did Attucks do?","Dockworker and sailor","Blacksmith and farmer","Teacher and printer","Soldier and lawyer",0],["Who was the first person killed at King Street?","Attucks","Samuel Adams","Paul Revere","John Adams",1],["How many men died in all?","Five","Two","Eight","Twelve",1],["What was the date of the Boston Massacre?","March 5, 1770","April 19, 1775","July 4, 1776","December 16, 1773",2],["Who defended the soldiers at their trial?","John Adams","Paul Revere","Samuel Adams","Thomas Jefferson",2],["Who remembered Attucks in the 1800s to show Black people helped win freedom?","Abolitionists","British generals","Boston bankers","Southern planters",3],["What do historians still debate?","His exact role that night","The year of the trial","Which town it happened in","Whether soldiers were arrested",3],["Why do we know little about his words or plans?","Few records survive","He wrote a long diary","He moved to England","He became a general",4],["Who kept telling his story over the years?","Writers, artists and speakers","Only British officers","Only his family","Boston judges",4]]}]},{"id":"kwashington","name":"Kenny Washington","years":"1918-1971","era":"past","tagline":"UCLA star who helped bring Black players back to the NFL in 1946","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Kenny Washington Opens the Door","paras":["Kenny Washington was born on August 31, 1918, in Los Angeles. He was a great runner and passer at UCLA. His teammates there included Jackie Robinson and Woody Strode. Both became famous too. Washington was named an All-American in 1939. UCLA later retired his number 13.","On March 21, 1946, the Los Angeles Rams signed Washington. He was the first Black player to sign an NFL contract in the modern era. Woody Strode joined the team a few weeks later. Washington played three seasons for the Rams. He did it on knees that had needed surgery.","The NFL had no Black players after 1933. Washington was still a star in the minor leagues in the early 1940s. He signed with the Rams in March 1946. In 1947, Jackie Robinson began playing for the Brooklyn Dodgers. Washington retired in 1948. He died in 1971.","For about 12 years, the NFL kept out Black players. It was never an official rule. The Rams had just moved to Los Angeles and needed to use the Coliseum. They were told they could lose their lease unless they signed Black players. Washington broke the barrier more than a year before Jackie Robinson did with the Brooklyn Dodgers in baseball.","Washington had skill and courage. He averaged 6.1 yards per carry in the NFL even with his bad knees. He joined the College Football Hall of Fame in 1956. After football, he worked as a Los Angeles police officer and later scouted for the Dodgers. Takeaway for an athlete: being first takes talent and nerve."],"remember":"Kenny Washington's 1946 signing helped end the NFL's ban on Black players.","quiz":[["In which city was Kenny Washington born?","Los Angeles","Chicago","Atlanta","Cleveland",0],["Which famous baseball player was his UCLA teammate?","Jackie Robinson","Hank Aaron","Willie Mays","Satchel Paige",0],["Which team signed Washington in 1946?","Los Angeles Rams","Chicago Bears","Detroit Lions","Cleveland Browns",1],["How many seasons did Washington play for the Rams?","three","one","six","nine",1],["After which year did the NFL have no Black players?","1933","1920","1941","1946",2],["In which year did Washington retire?","1948","1946","1950","1952",2],["What did the Rams risk losing if they did not sign Black players?","their lease on the Coliseum","their team name","their television deal","their best coach",3],["Which team did Jackie Robinson play for in 1947?","Brooklyn Dodgers","New York Yankees","Boston Red Sox","Chicago Cubs",3],["How many yards per carry did Washington average in the NFL?","6.1","3.2","4.5","8.0",4],["What job did Washington have after football?","police officer","school principal","car dealer","radio host",4]]}]},{"id":"franklin","name":"Aretha Franklin","years":"1942-2018","era":"past","tagline":"The Queen of Soul, whose powerful voice made her a music legend","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Aretha Franklin: Queen of Soul","paras":["Aretha Franklin was born on March 25, 1942, in Memphis, Tennessee. Her father, C. L. Franklin, was a well-known Baptist preacher. The family moved to Detroit, Michigan, when she was young. Even as a child, her talent stood out as she sang gospel music at her father's church and played piano by ear.","Aretha Franklin became known as the Queen of Soul. Her huge, powerful voice mixed gospel, rhythm and blues, and pop. Her version of Respect, released in 1967, became a number-one hit, and she won 18 Grammy Awards in her career. Her songs still play around the world today.","At age 14, Aretha recorded her first songs at her father's church. In 1960, she moved to New York City and signed with Columbia Records. She joined Atlantic Records in 1966, and her 1967 hits made her a superstar. In 1987, she became the first woman inducted into the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame, and she died on August 16, 2018.","In the 1960s, Black Americans were fighting for civil rights, and Aretha's music became part of that moment. Her version of Respect, sung with pride and power, became an anthem for the civil rights and women's movements. Her father was a friend of Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. She showed that Black women's voices could lead American music.","Aretha's power began in gospel church music, where she learned to sing from the heart. She sang with her sisters Carolyn and Erma, and she trusted her own instincts in the studio. In 2005, she received the Presidential Medal of Freedom. Takeaway for an athlete: build a strong foundation first, then trust it under pressure."],"remember":"Aretha Franklin turned gospel roots into soul music so strong that the world called her the Queen of Soul.","quiz":[["In which city was Aretha Franklin born?","Memphis, Tennessee","Nashville, Tennessee","Atlanta, Georgia","Chicago, Illinois",0],["What was her father's job?","A Baptist preacher","A jazz drummer","A school principal","A radio host",0],["What nickname did Aretha become known by?","The Queen of Soul","The Queen of Jazz","The First Lady of Gospel","The Queen of Pop",1],["How many Grammy Awards did she win?","18","8","12","25",1],["Which record label did she join in 1966?","Atlantic Records","Motown Records","Stax Records","Capitol Records",2],["What milestone did she reach in 1987?","First woman inducted into the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame","First woman to host a TV talk show","First woman to own a record label","First woman to sing at a World Series",2],["What did her version of Respect become?","An anthem for civil rights and women's movements","A theme song for a national football team","A hymn that was only sung in churches","A lullaby sung to children at bedtime",3],["Which civil rights leader was a friend of Aretha's father?","Dr. Martin Luther King Jr.","Frederick Douglass","Booker T. Washington","Harriet Tubman",3],["Where did Aretha's power begin?","In gospel church music","In opera training","In marching band practice","In radio talent shows",4],["Who sang with Aretha?","Her sisters Carolyn and Erma","Her brothers and cousins","Her father's church choir","Her school friends from Detroit",4]]}]},{"id":"bridges","name":"Ruby Bridges","years":"born 1954","era":"current","tagline":"At age six she walked into an all-white school and changed America","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"A Six-Year-Old's Brave Walk","paras":["Ruby Bridges was born on September 8, 1954, in Tylertown, Mississippi. When she was four, her family moved to New Orleans, Louisiana. Her parents, Abon and Lucille Bridges, hoped for better jobs and schools. She was born the same year the Supreme Court said that segregated public schools were wrong.","In 1960, at age six, Ruby became the first Black student at William Frantz Elementary School in New Orleans. The school had only white students before. Her walk to school became famous across the country. She went every day, even when angry crowds shouted at her.","A federal judge ordered New Orleans schools to begin desegregating, and in 1960 Ruby passed a test to enter the school. On November 14, 1960, four federal marshals escorted her and her mother to the building. She spent that first day in the principal's office. She finished first grade there that school year.","The Supreme Court ruled in 1954, but many Southern schools stayed segregated for years. Angry crowds gathered outside Ruby's school, and many white families kept their children home. Her father lost his job, and the family faced hard times. Ruby's courage helped push the country to open schools to all children.","Ruby had her family and a few caring adults. Her teacher, Barbara Henry, taught her alone in a classroom for the whole year, and child psychiatrist Robert Coles met with her to help her cope. She never missed a day of school, and in 1999 she started the Ruby Bridges Foundation to teach tolerance. Takeaway for an athlete: courage means showing up every day, even when it is hard, and leaning on your team."],"remember":"Ruby Bridges showed that courage means showing up, even at age six, and that one person can change a school.","quiz":[["Where was Ruby Bridges born?","Tylertown, Mississippi","New Orleans, Louisiana","Boston, Massachusetts","Montgomery, Alabama",0],["How old was Ruby when her family moved to New Orleans?","Four","Two","Six","Nine",0],["Which school did Ruby attend in 1960?","William Frantz Elementary School","Booker T. Washington School","Little Rock Central School","Dillard Elementary School",1],["How old was Ruby in 1960?","Six","Four","Nine","Twelve",1],["On what date did Ruby first enter the school?","November 14, 1960","November 14, 1954","September 8, 1960","November 4, 1961",2],["Where did she spend her first day?","In the principal's office","In the cafeteria","On the playground","In a classroom",2],["In what year did the Supreme Court rule on segregated schools?","1954","1948","1960","1964",3],["What did many white families do?","Kept their children home","Sent them to her class","Joined her at school","Moved away from the city",3],["Who was Ruby's teacher?","Barbara Henry","Lucille Bridges","Norma Jean","Eleanor Roosevelt",4],["In what year did she start the Ruby Bridges Foundation?","1999","1960","1964","1995",4]]}]},{"id":"bath","name":"Dr. Patricia Bath","years":"1942-2019","era":"past","tagline":"An eye doctor who invented a laser tool to restore sight and fought blindness.","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Dr. Patricia Bath: Saving Sight","paras":["Patricia Bath was born on November 4, 1942, in Harlem, New York. Her father, Rupert Bath, was the first Black motorman in the New York City subway system. Her mother, Gladys, worked as a domestic worker. Patricia did very well in school. In high school she won a science grant for a summer program.","Dr. Bath became an ophthalmologist, a doctor who treats eye problems. She co-founded the American Institute for the Prevention of Blindness to help people who could not get eye care. She also invented the Laserphaco Probe, a laser tool to remove cataracts. In 1988, she became the first Black woman doctor to receive a medical patent.","Patricia earned her bachelor's degree at Hunter College in 1964. She finished medical school at Howard University in 1968. In 1975, she became the first woman faculty member in ophthalmology at UCLA's Jules Stein Eye Institute. In 1983, she became the first woman to lead an ophthalmology residency program. She died on May 30, 2019.","Many people in poor communities lost their sight because they could not get care. Bath called her answer community ophthalmology. It meant bringing eye care to the people who needed it. Her laser device helped restore sight to many patients. She also opened doors for women and Black doctors in medicine.","Bath faced many closed doors but kept going with hard work and strong grades. She began working on the Laserphaco Probe in 1981 and spent almost five years developing it. She filed for her patent in 1986, and it was granted in 1988. Takeaway for an athlete: big goals take years of steady work, so keep improving."],"remember":"Patricia Bath turned a big idea into a laser tool that helps people see again.","quiz":[["What was Patricia's father's job?","A subway motorman","A postal worker","A schoolteacher","A doctor",0],["In which New York neighborhood was she born?","Harlem","Brooklyn","The Bronx","Queens",0],["What is a doctor who treats eye problems called?","An ophthalmologist","A cardiologist","A dermatologist","A neurologist",1],["What does the Laserphaco Probe remove?","Cataracts","Tumors","Tonsils","Gallstones",1],["At which school did she earn her medical degree?","Howard University","Hunter College","Yale University","Columbia University",2],["In which year did she become the first woman to lead an ophthalmology residency program?","1983","1973","1993","1968",2],["What did Bath call her answer to the lack of eye care?","Community ophthalmology","Mobile surgery","Neighborhood optics","Free vision plan",3],["Why did many people in poor communities lose their sight?","They could not get care","Their schools had no eye tests","They refused treatment","They spent too long outdoors",3],["How long did Bath spend developing the Laserphaco Probe?","Almost five years","About five months","Almost ten years","About two years",4],["In which year did she begin working on the probe?","1981","1971","1976","1991",4]]}]},{"id":"henson","name":"Matthew Henson","years":"1866-1955","era":"past","tagline":"Arctic explorer who stood beside Peary at the top of the world","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Matthew Henson in the Arctic","paras":["Matthew Henson was born on August 8, 1866, in Charles County, Maryland. He was orphaned as a boy. At about age twelve, he went to sea as a cabin boy on a sailing ship called the Katie Hines. He spent years working on ships and learning the sailor's trade.","Henson became the key assistant to explorer Robert Peary. He learned the Inuit language and became an expert at driving dog sleds. On April 6, 1909, Henson, Peary and four Inuit men traveled to what Peary believed was the North Pole. Henson planted the U.S. flag there. Some people still debate whether they reached the exact spot.","Peary hired Henson in 1887 as an assistant. From 1891 to 1909, Henson served on seven Arctic expeditions. In 1912, he wrote a book about the North Pole trip. In 1913, President William Howard Taft gave him a job at the New York Customs House. Henson died on March 9, 1955.","The Arctic is bitterly cold and dangerous. Henson's skills with the Inuit language and dog sleds helped the team survive. Yet because of racial prejudice, his role was largely overlooked for years, and Peary received most of the fame. His story shows how much Black explorers gave to discovery.","Henson learned by doing, from the sea to the ice. He learned from his Inuit teammates and worked hard every day. In 1988, the government reburied him next to Peary at Arlington National Cemetery. Takeaway for an athlete: do your job well and learn from everyone on the team, even when no one cheers."],"remember":"Matthew Henson's skill and hard work made him a key part of Peary's Arctic trips.","quiz":[["What was the name of the ship Henson sailed on as a cabin boy?","Katie Hines","Mayflower","Santa Maria","Silver Star",0],["About how old was Henson when he went to sea?","Twelve","Eight","Sixteen","Twenty",0],["What skill did Henson become an expert at?","Driving dog sleds","Flying small planes","Building stone houses","Drawing maps",1],["How many Inuit men traveled with Henson and Peary in 1909?","Four","Two","Six","Ten",1],["In what year did Peary hire Henson?","1887","1891","1909","1913",2],["Which president gave Henson a Customs House job?","William Howard Taft","Theodore Roosevelt","Woodrow Wilson","Calvin Coolidge",2],["Why was Henson's role overlooked for years?","Racial prejudice","He left the trip early","He kept it secret","He lost his maps",3],["Who received most of the fame?","Peary","Henson","President Taft","An Inuit hunter",3],["Where was Henson reburied in 1988?","Arlington National Cemetery","Boston Common","A church in Charles County","The Customs House",4],["Who did Henson learn from, according to the lesson?","His Inuit teammates","Navy admirals","Peary's rivals","Ship's captains",4]]}]},{"id":"anderson","name":"Marian Anderson","years":"1897-1993","era":"past","tagline":"Great contralto who sang for 75,000 people at the Lincoln Memorial","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Marian Anderson: A Voice That Opened Doors","paras":["Marian Anderson was born on February 27, 1897, in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania. She was the oldest of three sisters. At age six, she joined the junior choir at Union Baptist Church, and her church community later helped raise money for her music lessons. She had a rare, deep singing voice called a contralto.","Anderson became one of the greatest concert singers of the 1900s. She sang in famous halls across America and Europe. On January 7, 1955, she became the first African American to sing at the Metropolitan Opera in New York. She sang the role of Ulrica in Verdi's opera A Masked Ball.","In 1939, the Daughters of the American Revolution refused to let Anderson sing at Constitution Hall in Washington, D.C. The hall did not allow Black performers. On Easter Sunday, April 9, 1939, she sang at the Lincoln Memorial instead. In 1963, she received the Presidential Medal of Freedom. She died on April 8, 1993, at age 96.","The refusal made news across the country. First Lady Eleanor Roosevelt resigned from the group in protest and helped arrange the Lincoln Memorial concert. More than 75,000 people came, and millions more listened on the radio. The concert became a landmark moment in the fight against segregation.","Anderson worked with demanding teachers and practiced for years. She studied and sang in Europe in the early 1930s, and her manager, Sol Hurok, helped her book concerts starting in 1934. She stayed calm and dignified under pressure. Takeaway for an athlete: when the crowd or the moment gets loud, let your preparation speak."],"remember":"Marian Anderson answered unfair rules with calm, great singing, and her Lincoln Memorial concert inspired the nation.","quiz":[["In which city was Marian Anderson born?","Philadelphia, Pennsylvania","Baltimore, Maryland","Boston, Massachusetts","Richmond, Virginia",0],["At what age did she join her church's junior choir?","At age six","At age three","At age ten","At age fourteen",0],["At which opera house did she become the first African American to sing in 1955?","The Metropolitan Opera","La Scala in Milan","The Vienna State Opera","The Royal Opera House",1],["Which role did she sing there?","The role of Ulrica","The role of Carmen","The role of Aida","The role of Madame Butterfly",1],["Which group refused to let her sing at Constitution Hall?","The Daughters of the American Revolution","The Metropolitan Opera board","The United States Senate","The Washington Symphony",2],["Where did she sing on Easter Sunday in 1939?","The Lincoln Memorial","Arlington Cemetery","Carnegie Hall","The Washington Monument",2],["Who resigned from the group in protest?","Eleanor Roosevelt","Mary McLeod Bethune","Amelia Earhart","Helen Keller",3],["About how many people came to the concert?","More than 75,000","More than 7,500","More than 25,000","More than 150,000",3],["Who was her manager starting in 1934?","Sol Hurok","Harold Ickes","Leonard Bernstein","Paul Robeson",4],["How did Anderson act under pressure?","Calm and dignified","Angry and loud","Nervous and rushed","Silent and absent",4]]}]},{"id":"dean","name":"Mark Dean","years":"born 1957","era":"current","tagline":"He helped design the ISA bus and became IBM's first African American Fellow","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Mark Dean and the PC","paras":["Mark Dean was born on March 2, 1957, in Jefferson City, Tennessee. He studied electrical engineering, which is the science of electricity and electronic parts. He earned his first degree at the University of Tennessee in 1979. He then earned a master's degree at Florida Atlantic University and a PhD at Stanford University.","Dean became a computer engineer at IBM. With fellow engineer Dennis Moeller, he helped design the ISA bus. A bus is a pathway inside a computer that lets parts such as printers and monitors connect to it. Dean also holds three of the nine core patents behind IBM's first personal computer.","Dean joined IBM in 1980. In the early 1980s, he and Moeller worked out the ISA bus, and the patent for it was granted in 1985. In 1995, Dean became an IBM Fellow, the first African American to earn that top honor. In 1997, he was added to the National Inventors Hall of Fame.","In the early days, personal computers were new and hard to build. The ISA bus gave makers a standard way to plug parts into a PC. That helped printers, keyboards and monitors work together. Dean's rise to IBM Fellow also showed young Black engineers what was possible in the tech world.","Dean studied hard for many years, from his first degree all the way to his PhD. He also worked as part of a team, and the ISA bus came from sharing ideas with Dennis Moeller. His work helped shape the personal computer era. Takeaway for an athlete: great results come from teamwork and steady practice."],"remember":"Mark Dean helped design the ISA bus and became IBM's first African American Fellow.","quiz":[["Where was Mark Dean born?","Jefferson City, Tennessee","Atlanta, Georgia","Austin, Texas","Raleigh, North Carolina",0],["Which school gave Dean his PhD?","Stanford University","Howard University","Georgia Tech","Duke University",0],["Who helped Dean design the ISA bus?","Dennis Moeller","Steve Wozniak","Gordon Moore","Robert Noyce",1],["How many of the nine core patents behind IBM's first PC does Dean hold?","Three","One","Five","Seven",1],["In what year did Dean join IBM?","1980","1975","1990","1995",2],["What honor did Dean receive in 1995?","IBM Fellow","Chief executive of IBM","Stanford professorship","A Nobel Prize",2],["The ISA bus helped which devices work together?","Printers, keyboards and monitors","Phones, cameras and radios","Clocks, speakers and TVs","Games, books and maps",3],["What did the ISA bus give makers?","A standard way to plug parts into a PC","A cheaper way to ship computers","A secret code to protect data","A faster way to sell PCs",3],["What did Dean do for many years, from his first degree to his PhD?","Studied hard","Played pro sports","Ran a company","Taught gym class",4],["What two things does the athlete takeaway say bring great results?","Teamwork and steady practice","Luck and shortcuts","Size and speed","Money and fame",4]]}]},{"id":"pollard","name":"Fritz Pollard","years":"1894-1986","era":"past","tagline":"Brown star who became the first Black head coach in the NFL","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Fritz Pollard: First Black Head Coach","paras":["Frederick Douglass \"Fritz\" Pollard was born on January 27, 1894, in Chicago. His full name was Frederick Douglass Pollard. He was a swift halfback. He went to Brown University in Rhode Island and helped it reach the Rose Bowl game. He was named an All-American in 1916.","Pollard joined the Akron Pros in 1920. He and Bobby Marshall were the first two Black players in the NFL. Akron went undefeated and won the league's first championship that year. In 1921, Pollard became co-head coach of Akron. He was the first Black head coach in NFL history.","Pollard played pro football from 1920 to 1926. In 1921, he became co-coach with Elgie Tobin. He also played for the Milwaukee Badgers, Hammond Pros and Providence Steam Roller. In 1928, he formed an all-Black team called the Chicago Black Hawks. The NFL had no Black players after 1933.","In the early NFL, Pollard faced racism from fans and some opponents. He kept playing and coaching anyway. It was not until 1989 that the NFL hired another Black head coach, Art Shell of the Raiders. Today, the Fritz Pollard Alliance, a group that supports minority coaches and staff, carries his name.","Pollard led by example. He was a smart player and also worked as a coach, so he knew the game from both sides. Later, he published a newspaper and worked in business. He joined the Pro Football Hall of Fame in 2005. Takeaway for an athlete: lead by how you play, even when you are the only one."],"remember":"Fritz Pollard played and coached at the highest level when few would let him.","quiz":[["In which city was Fritz Pollard born?","Chicago","Boston","Akron","Providence",0],["Which university did Pollard attend?","Brown University","Harvard University","Yale University","Ohio State",0],["Which NFL team did Pollard join in 1920?","Akron Pros","Chicago Bears","Hammond Pros","Green Bay Packers",1],["What did Akron win in 1920?","the league's first championship","a Rose Bowl trophy","the Brown Bombers cup","a national poll",1],["Who was Pollard's co-coach in 1921?","Elgie Tobin","Art Shell","Bobby Marshall","Jim Thorpe",2],["What all-Black team did Pollard form in 1928?","Chicago Black Hawks","Brown Bombers","Akron Stars","Providence Giants",2],["Who became the next Black NFL head coach in 1989?","Art Shell","Tony Dungy","Elgie Tobin","Doug Williams",3],["Which group today carries Pollard's name?","Fritz Pollard Alliance","Akron Coaches Club","Rose Bowl Society","Brown Boosters",3],["What did Pollard publish later in life?","a newspaper","a cookbook","a coaching manual","a sports magazine",4],["In which year did Pollard join the Pro Football Hall of Fame?","2005","1971","1993","2016",4]]}]},{"id":"davis","name":"Benjamin O. Davis Jr.","years":"1912-2002","era":"past","tagline":"He led the Tuskegee Airmen and later earned a fourth star","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Leading the Tuskegee Airmen","paras":["Benjamin O. Davis Jr. was born on December 18, 1912, in Washington, D.C. His father, Benjamin O. Davis Sr., was the first African American general in any branch of the U.S. military. The younger Davis entered West Point in 1932 and graduated in 1936. He was the fourth African American to graduate from the academy.","Davis led the Black fighter pilots later known as the Tuskegee Airmen. He took command of the 99th Fighter Squadron in August 1942. In fall 1943, he took command of the 332nd Fighter Group. His pilots flew in Italy and protected bomber planes. On March 24, 1945, their mission guarding bombers headed to Berlin earned a Distinguished Unit Citation.","Davis was born in 1912 and finished West Point in 1936. He earned his pilot wings in March 1942. In 1948, he helped plan the Air Force's move to end segregation. He retired as a lieutenant general in 1970. On December 9, 1998, President Bill Clinton promoted him to four-star general. Davis died on July 4, 2002, at age 89.","In the 1940s, the U.S. military was segregated. Some leaders doubted that Black men could fly fighter planes. Davis and his airmen set out to prove the doubters wrong. Their success helped build the case for ending segregation in the armed forces and mattered for the whole country.","Davis set high standards and kept his pilots focused on the mission. His leadership earned lasting respect, and in 1998 he finally received his fourth star. Many people still see him as a model of discipline and pride. Takeaway for an athlete: hold yourself to high standards, because a strong team can silence the doubters."],"remember":"Benjamin O. Davis Jr. led the Tuskegee Airmen and proved Black pilots could excel.","quiz":[["What was special about Davis's father?","He was the first African American general","He founded the Air Force","He was a famous test pilot","He taught at West Point",0],["In what year did Davis graduate from West Point?","1936","1942","1948","1953",0],["What earned the 332nd a Distinguished Unit Citation?","Guarding bombers headed to Berlin","Training new pilots at Tuskegee","Flying supplies to Paris","Bombing a bridge in Rome",1],["Which group did Davis lead starting in fall 1943?","The 332nd Fighter Group","The 477th Composite Group","The Eighth Air Force","The Flying Tigers",1],["When did Davis earn his pilot wings?","March 1942","August 1943","May 1945","June 1938",2],["Who promoted Davis to four-star general?","President Bill Clinton","President Harry Truman","President Franklin Roosevelt","President Dwight Eisenhower",2],["What was the U.S. military like in the 1940s?","Segregated","Fully integrated","Closed to pilots","Run by Congress",3],["What did Davis and his airmen set out to do?","Prove the doubters wrong","Break speed records","Start their own air force","Win air shows",3],["What did Davis keep his pilots focused on?","The mission","Medals","Newspaper stories","Promotions",4],["What does the takeaway say a strong team can do?","Silence the doubters","Skip practice","Avoid hard games","Replace the coach",4]]}]},{"id":"hurston","name":"Zora Neale Hurston","years":"1891-1960","era":"past","tagline":"Writer and folklorist who captured Black Southern voices on the page","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Zora Neale Hurston: Collector of Stories","paras":["Zora Neale Hurston was born on January 7, 1891, in Notasulga, Alabama. When she was a small child, her family moved to Eatonville, Florida. Eatonville was one of the first towns in America run by Black citizens. Zora grew up hearing local storytellers swap tall tales and jokes.","Hurston became a leading writer of the Harlem Renaissance, a burst of Black art and writing in the 1920s. She wrote short stories, plays, and novels. Her most famous novel is Their Eyes Were Watching God. She was also a folklorist, someone who collects the tales and songs of everyday people.","In 1925, Hurston began studying at Barnard College in New York City. She worked with the anthropologist Franz Boas and graduated in 1928. Her folklore book Mules and Men came out in 1935, and Their Eyes Were Watching God followed in 1937. She died on January 28, 1960, in Fort Pierce, Florida.","In Hurston's time, many people looked down on Black Southern speech and folktales. Hurston saw them as rich art. She wrote the way real people talked and took pride in Black culture. Her work helped save stories that might have been lost, and today her books are seen as classics.","Hurston traveled through the South to collect songs and stories in person, listening closely and writing down what she heard. She wrote Their Eyes Were Watching God in about seven weeks. In the 1970s, writer Alice Walker helped bring readers back to her work. Takeaway for an athlete: learn by studying closely, like film study before a game."],"remember":"Zora Neale Hurston saw the art in everyday Black Southern life and wrote it down so it would never be lost.","quiz":[["Where was Zora Neale Hurston born?","Notasulga, Alabama","Montgomery, Alabama","Savannah, Georgia","New Orleans, Louisiana",0],["To which town did her family move when she was small?","Eatonville, Florida","Tampa, Florida","Mobile, Alabama","Macon, Georgia",0],["What is her most famous novel?","Their Eyes Were Watching God","Native Son","Invisible Man","Cane",1],["What is a folklorist?","Someone who collects the tales and songs of everyday people","Someone who writes new songs for the radio","Someone who builds and runs historic museums","Someone who teaches foreign languages to adults",1],["In what year did Hurston graduate from Barnard?","1928","1925","1935","1937",2],["Where did she die?","Fort Pierce, Florida","Eatonville, Florida","New York City","Montgomery, Alabama",2],["How did Hurston see Black Southern speech and folktales?","As rich art","As simple jokes","As habits to fix","As school lessons",3],["What did her work help save?","Stories that might have been lost","Old buildings in Eatonville","Recipes from her family","Letters from soldiers",3],["About how long did she take to write Their Eyes Were Watching God?","About seven weeks","About seven days","About seven months","About seven years",4],["Which writer helped bring readers back to Hurston's work in the 1970s?","Alice Walker","Maya Angelou","Toni Morrison","Langston Hughes",4]]}]},{"id":"dungy","name":"Tony Dungy","years":"born 1955","era":"current","tagline":"Calm coach who led the Colts to Super Bowl XLI and made history","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Tony Dungy: Quiet Strength","paras":["Tony Dungy was born on October 6, 1955, in Jackson, Michigan. He played quarterback at the University of Minnesota. After college, no team drafted him. The Pittsburgh Steelers signed him anyway and moved him to defensive back. He was part of the Steelers' Super Bowl XIII win.","Dungy became an NFL head coach and led the Indianapolis Colts to a 29-17 win over the Chicago Bears in Super Bowl XLI. He was the first Black head coach to win a Super Bowl. He is known for staying calm and treating players with respect. He won 139 regular-season games.","After playing, Dungy coached for the Steelers, Kansas City Chiefs and Minnesota Vikings. In 1996, the Tampa Bay Buccaneers hired him as head coach. They fired him in January 2002. The Colts hired him eight days later. The Colts won Super Bowl XLI in 2007. He retired after 2008. He entered the Hall of Fame in 2016.","Dungy's win was a big moment. Bears coach Lovie Smith was also Black, so two Black head coaches met in a Super Bowl for the first time. Dungy also reached people outside football. He wrote a book called Quiet Strength. He also became a spokesperson for All Pro Dad, which encourages fathers.","Dungy led with patience, not yelling. He trusted his players and kept his focus on doing things right. His Colts made the playoffs in all seven seasons he coached them. After coaching, he became an analyst for NBC. Takeaway for an athlete: you can win while staying calm and respectful."],"remember":"Tony Dungy showed that calm, respectful leadership can win a Super Bowl.","quiz":[["In which state was Tony Dungy born?","Michigan","Ohio","Indiana","Florida",0],["Which position did Dungy play at Minnesota?","quarterback","linebacker","running back","kicker",0],["Which team did the Colts beat in Super Bowl XLI?","Chicago Bears","New England Patriots","Dallas Cowboys","Seattle Seahawks",1],["What was the Super Bowl XLI score?","29-17","42-10","35-31","38-34",1],["In which year did the Buccaneers hire Dungy as head coach?","1996","1989","2002","2008",2],["How many days after Tampa Bay fired him did the Colts hire him?","eight","two","fifteen","thirty",2],["Which Bears head coach also made history in Super Bowl XLI?","Lovie Smith","Mike Ditka","Art Shell","Joe Gibbs",3],["What is the title of Dungy's book?","Quiet Strength","Calm Leader","Winning Ways","Steady Hand",3],["How many seasons in a row did Dungy's Colts make the playoffs?","seven","two","four","eleven",4],["Which TV network did Dungy join as an analyst?","NBC","ESPN","CBS","FOX",4]]}]},{"id":"louverture","name":"Toussaint Louverture","years":"c. 1743-1803","era":"past","tagline":"Born enslaved, he led the Haitian Revolution that ended slavery in Saint-Domingue","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Toussaint Louverture and the Haitian Revolution","paras":["Toussaint Louverture was born into slavery around 1743 in Saint-Domingue. That was a French colony on the island of Hispaniola. Today it is Haiti. The colony grew sugar and coffee using the work of hundreds of thousands of enslaved people. Toussaint was freed in the 1770s, and he later became a leader.","Louverture became a top general and leader of the revolution of enslaved people in Saint-Domingue. He built a strong army of former slaves. In 1801, a new constitution named him Governor-General for Life. It also said slavery would never exist in the colony. He is called the father of Haiti.","A large revolt began in August 1791. At first, Louverture fought for Spain. In February 1794, France ended slavery, and he joined the French side. In 1802, Napoleon sent a French army to take back control. Louverture was arrested in June 1802 and sent to France. Haiti declared independence on January 1, 1804.","Saint-Domingue was one of the richest colonies in the world, thanks to its sugar and coffee. Its wealth came from forced labor, and the people who did the work had no freedom. Louverture fought to end this. After his death, Haiti became the first free Black republic in the world. It showed that enslaved people could win freedom.","Louverture was a smart planner and a skilled leader. He kept his army disciplined and used the land to his advantage. People said his name, which means \"the opening,\" came from his skill at finding openings in battle. He died at Fort de Joux in France on April 7, 1803, at age 59. General Jean-Jacques Dessalines carried on the fight. Takeaway for an athlete: great leaders plan ahead and find openings."],"remember":"Toussaint Louverture rose from slavery to lead a revolution that created free Haiti.","quiz":[["What was Haiti called when Louverture was born?","Saint-Domingue","New Orleans","Port-au-Prince","Santo Domingo",0],["What was Louverture's status at birth?","enslaved","a soldier","a priest","a plantation owner",0],["What title did the 1801 constitution give Louverture?","Governor-General for Life","King of Haiti","President of France","Emperor of Hispaniola",1],["What did the 1801 constitution say about slavery?","it would never exist in the colony","it would end in ten years","it could continue for farms","it needed a vote",1],["In which year did a large revolt begin?","1791","1763","1804","1815",2],["On what date did Haiti declare independence?","January 1, 1804","July 4, 1776","April 7, 1803","August 1, 1791",2],["What products made Saint-Domingue so rich?","sugar and coffee","corn and wheat","cotton and rice","tobacco and tea",3],["Haiti became the first what in the world?","free Black republic","Caribbean empire","French territory","island kingdom",3],["Where did Louverture die?","Fort de Joux","Paris","Port-au-Prince","Madrid",4],["Which general carried on the fight after Louverture?","Jean-Jacques Dessalines","Napoleon Bonaparte","Charles Leclerc","Georges Biassou",4]]}]},{"id":"maathai","name":"Wangari Maathai","years":"1940-2011","era":"past","tagline":"She planted a movement of trees and won the Nobel Peace Prize in 2004","days":[{"focus":"WHO · WHAT · WHEN · WHY · HOW","title":"Wangari Maathai and the Green Belt Movement","paras":["Wangari Maathai was born in 1940 in Nyeri, Kenya. She studied in the United States and earned a biology degree in Kansas and a master's degree in Pittsburgh. Back in Kenya, she earned a doctorate from the University of Nairobi. She was the first woman in East and Central Africa to earn a doctorate.","Maathai started the Green Belt Movement. It helped women in villages plant trees across the country of Kenya. The group helped plant more than 20 million trees. Women who planted trees could earn a little money to help their families. In 2004, she won the Nobel Peace Prize.","Maathai had the idea in 1976 and the Green Belt Movement grew from 1977. She was elected to Kenya's parliament in December 2002. She later served as Assistant Minister for Environment. She won the Nobel Peace Prize in 2004. She died on September 25, 2011, at age 71.","Forests in Kenya were being cut down. Women had to walk farther to find firewood and clean water. Soil washed away. Maathai saw that helping nature also helped families. She also spoke up for democracy and human rights. She was the first African woman to win the Nobel Peace Prize.","Maathai worked closely with village women. She taught them that small actions add up. She kept going even when the work was hard and the road was long. Today, the Green Belt Movement still plants trees. Takeaway for an athlete: small steps repeated every day can build something huge."],"remember":"Wangari Maathai showed that one person planting one tree at a time can change a country.","quiz":[["In which town was Wangari Maathai born?","Nyeri","Nairobi","Mombasa","Kisumu",0],["What was she the first woman in East and Central Africa to earn?","a doctorate","a law license","a pilot license","a medal",0],["What did the Green Belt Movement help women plant?","trees","wheat","flowers","coffee",1],["How many trees did the group help plant?","more than 20 million","about 5,000","over 2 billion","about 300,000",1],["In which year did Maathai win the Nobel Peace Prize?","2004","1977","2002","2011",2],["In which year did Maathai die?","2011","1976","2004","2008",2],["What did women have to walk farther to find?","firewood and clean water","schools and clinics","markets and jobs","roads and bridges",3],["What was Maathai the first African woman to win?","the Nobel Peace Prize","an Olympic medal","a Pulitzer Prize","a Kenyan election",3],["Who did Maathai work closely with?","village women","football coaches","bank leaders","foreign soldiers",4],["What does the Green Belt Movement still do today?","plants trees","builds stadiums","trains athletes","publishes books",4]]}]}];
